{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01869t1sdqsV3dYob3wqtUPn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t### class Input\n2\t\n3\tKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n4\t- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n5\t  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n6\t- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n7\t  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n8\t  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n9\t- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n10\t  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n11\t  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n12\t  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n13\t  a frame is not lost.\n14\t- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n15\t\n16\t- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n17\t- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n18\t- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it n\n19\t- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n20\t- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n21\t- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n22\t- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n23\t- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n24\t- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n25\t- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n26\t- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n27\t- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n28\t- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n29\t- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everyt\n30\t- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n31\t- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n32\t- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n33\t- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n34\t- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n35\t- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n36\t- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n37\t- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n38\t- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n39\t- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n40\t- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped,\n41\t- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n42\t- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n43\t- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n44\t- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n45\t- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n46\t- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n47\t- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n48\t- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n49\t- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n50\t- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n51\t- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n52\t- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. \n53\t- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the \n54\t- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n55\t- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n56\t- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n57\t- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n58\t- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n59\t- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n60\t\n61\t### class AudioManager\n62\t\n63\tWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\n64\tsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\n65\tpersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n66\t\n67\tBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\n68\tfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n69\t(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\n70\twas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n71\t\n72\t- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n73\t- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n74\t- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n75\t- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n76\t- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n77\t- `context: AudioContext | null`\n78\t- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n79\t- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n80\t- `get muted: boolean`\n81\t- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n82\t- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n83\t- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n84\t- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n85\t- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Aud\n86\t- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n87\t- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n88\t- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n89\t- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n90\t- `stopMusic(): void`\n91\t- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n92\t- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n93\t- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n94\t- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n95\t- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n96\t- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n97\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n98\t\n99\t### interface SoundDef\n100\t\n101\t- `wave: Wave`\n102\t- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n103\t- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n104\t- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n105\t- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n106\t- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n107\t- `sustain?: number`\n108\t- `decay?: number`\n109\t- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n110\t- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n111\t- `arpRate?: number`\n112\t- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n113\t- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n114\t- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n115\t- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n116\t\n117\t### interface Song\n118\t\n119\tA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\n120\twhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\n121\tEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\n122\tnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n123\t\n124\t- `bpm: number`\n125\t- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n126\t- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n127\t- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n128\t- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n129\t- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n130\t\n131\t### interface Voice\n132\t\n133\tA looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall.\n134\t\n135\t- `readonly name: string`\n136\t- `readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>`: The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them).\n137\t- `readonly sounding: boolean`: Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started.\n138\t- `readonly stopped: boolean`\n139\t- `set(s: VoiceSettings): void`: Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks).\n140\t- `stop(): void`\n141\t\n142\t### interface VoiceSettings\n143\t\n144\t- `volume?: number`: 0..1 (default 1).\n145\t- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n146\t- `muffle?: number`: 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down.\n147\t- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones.\n148\t\n149\t### spatialize()\n150\t\n151\t```ts\n152\tfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n153\t```\n154\t\n155\tGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n156\t\n157\t### class Hud\n158\t\n159\tPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\n160\tthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\n161\tlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n162\t(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n163\t\n164\tRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n165\t(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\n166\tresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n167\t\n168\t  ctx.hud.clear();\n169\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n170\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n171\t\n172\t- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n173\t- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n174\t- `visible: boolean`\n175\t- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n176\t- `get height: number`\n177\t- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n178\t- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n179\t- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n180\t- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n181\t- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n182\t- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n183\t- `dispose(): void`\n184\t\n185\t### class Particles\n186\t\n187\tPixel-art particles: CPU-simulated pools, drawn as camera-facing square sprites in flat\n188\tpalette colors. One emitter (one instanced draw call, one pooled instance buffer) per\n189\tpreset; sizes are whole art pixels, so in Pixel mode a particle is a crisp N×N block.\n190\tAvailable as `ctx.particles`; emitters are created on first use and freed by `clear()`\n191\t(the engine calls it when a level unloads).\n192\t\n193\t  ctx.particles.burst('dust', hero.feet);\n194\t  ctx.particles.burst('skid', feet, { direction: [-vx, 0.3, -vz], count: 2 });\n195\t\n196\t- `new Particles(view: () => ParticleView)`\n197\t- `readonly group: Group<Object3DEventMap>`: Engine-owned group holding every emitter.\n198\t- `readonly presets: Map<string, ParticlePreset>`: Preset registry: the built-ins plus anything registered.\n199\t- `readonly pixelWorld: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`: World units per art pixel at the camera focus (updated every frame).\n200\t- `register(name: string, preset: ParticlePreset): void`: Add or replace a named preset. Replacing frees the old preset's emitter.\n201\t- `burst(preset: string | ParticlePreset, at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number], options: BurstOptions = {}): number`: Spawn a burst of `preset` (a name or a ParticlePreset) at `at`. Returns the number of particles spawned (fewer when the pool is ful\n202\t- `get alive: number`: Particles alive right now, over all emitters.\n203\t- `get emitterCount: number`: Emitters (= draw calls at most) currently allocated.\n204\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Simulate and upload. Called by the engine every frame (frozen while paused).\n205\t- `reseed(rng: Rng): void`: Take bursts' randomness from `rng` (the engine passes each level's `ctx.random` fork).\n206\t- `clear(): void`: Kill every particle and free the emitters (GPU buffers, materials).\n207\t- `dispose(): void`\n208\t\n209\t### interface ParticlePreset\n210\t\n211\tA particle effect as data. Sizes are in art pixels (whole pixels, so particles stay crisp\n212\tsquares), colors are palette names stepped over each particle's life (no blending).\n213\t\n214\t- `count: Range`: Particles per burst.\n215\t- `life: Range`: Seconds each particle lives.\n216\t- `speed: Range`: Launch speed, world units / s.\n217\t- `direction?: Vec3`: Main launch direction (default straight up).\n218\t- `spread?: number`: Cone half-angle around `direction`, degrees (180 = every direction). Default 180.\n219\t- `flatten?: number`: Multiply vertical launch speed (0.2 = dust that hugs the ground). Default 1.\n220\t- `gravity?: number`: Downward acceleration, world units / s² (negative floats up). Default 0.\n221\t- `drag?: number`: Velocity damping per second (0 = none).\n222\t- `size: readonly [number, number]`: Size in art pixels at birth and at death (rounded to whole pixels).\n223\t- `colors: readonly PaletteColor[]`: Palette colors stepped through over the lifetime.\n224\t- `radius?: number`: Random spawn offset radius, world units.\n225\t- `max?: number`: Pool size (max alive at once). Default 256.\n226\t\n227\t### class Terrain\n228\t\n229\t- `new Terrain(o: TerrainOptions = {})`\n230\t- `readonly nx: number`\n231\t- `readonly nz: number`\n232\t- `readonly sizeX: number`\n233\t- `readonly sizeZ: number`\n234\t- `readonly at: readonly [number, number, number]`\n235\t- `readonly heights: Float32Array`: Heights at the grid points, row by row along z: `heights[ix + iz * (nx + 1)]`.\n236\t- `readonly mesh: Mesh`\n237\t- `collider: RAPIER.Collider | null`\n238\t- `get cell: [number, number]`: Metres between grid points along x and z.\n239\t- `pointX(ix: number): number`: World x, z of grid point (ix, iz).\n240\t- `pointZ(iz: number): number`\n241\t- `generate(f: (x: number, z: number) => number): this`: Fill the heights from `f(x, z)` (world x, z; metres above the terrain's origin), then redraw.\n242\t- `heightAt(x: number, z: number): number`: Height of the surface at world (x, z), in world y: the triangle it is over, as the mesh and the collider have it. Outside the grid: its nearest edge.\n243\t- `normalAt(x: number, z: number, out = new Vector3()): Vector3`: The surface's normal at world (x, z) (central differences over half a cell).\n244\t- `update(): void`: Redraw the mesh from the heights (positions, flat normals, colours per face).\n245\t- `attach(physics: Physics): RAPIER.Collider`: Make (or remake, after `generate` / `erode`) the heightfield collider: a fixed body at the terrain's origin. Returns it.\n246\t- `detach(): void`: Remove the collider (the mesh stays).\n247\t- `dispose(): void`: The collider and the mesh's geometry go (the shared material stays).\n248\t- `erode(o: ErosionOptions = {}): void`: Hydraulic erosion: `droplets` raindrops, each rolling downhill from a random point. A drop carries soil up to a capacity that grows with its speed, water and how steeply it is falling: below capacity it digs (over a sma\n249\t\n250\t### interface TerrainOptions\n251\t\n252\t- `size?: readonly [number, number]`: Size in metres along x and z (default [32, 32]).\n253\t- `cells?: readonly [number, number]`: Cells along x and z (default one per metre).\n254\t- `at?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Where the middle of the grid's height 0 is (default the origin).\n255\t- `bands?: readonly TerrainBand[]`: Colour by height, lowest first; the last band takes everything above.\n256\t- `steep?: number`: Faces steeper than this (their normal's y below it) are rock. Default 0.72 (about 44°).\n257\t- `rock?: number`\n258\t\n259\t### TERRAIN_BANDS\n260\t\n261\t```ts\n262\tconst TERRAIN_BANDS: readonly TerrainBand[]\n263\t```\n264\t\n265\tSea, beach, grass, forest, rock, snow (the engine palette).\n266\t\n267\t### class GrassField\n268\t\n269\t- `new GrassField(o: GrassOptions)`\n270\t- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind along x and z (its length is the strength).\n271\t- `sway: number`: How far blades lean per unit of wind (m at the tip). Default 0.12.\n272\t- `readonly blades: number`: Blades actually placed (the mask can leave fewer than `count`).\n273\t- `push(i: number, at: Vector3Like | null, radius = 0.8): void`: Pusher `i` (0..3): blades within `radius` of `at` part and flatten (radius 0: none).\n274\t- `update(time: number): void`: Per frame (game time).\n275\t- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n276\t\n277\t### interface GrassOptions\n278\t\n279\t- `area: readonly [number, number]`: Width (x) and depth (z) of the patch, m.\n280\t- `at: readonly [number, number, number]`: Centre of the patch on the ground.\n281\t- `count: number`\n282\t- `height?: readonly [number, number]`: Blade heights (m): shortest and tallest. Default [0.25, 0.55].\n283\t- `width?: number`: Blade width at the root (m). Default 0.07.\n284\t- `base?: number`: Colours (hex): roots and tips.\n285\t- `tip?: number`\n286\t- `mask?: (x: number, z: number) => boolean`: Where blades grow (world x, z); default everywhere in the patch.\n287\t- `ground?: (x: number, z: number) => number`: Ground height under a blade (default: the patch's y).\n288\t- `seed?: number`\n289\t\n290\t### class SpriteBatch\n291\t\n292\t- `new SpriteBatch(sheet: SpriteSheet, o: { capacity: number })`\n293\t- `readonly sheet: SpriteSheet`\n294\t- `spawn(at: readonly [number, number, number], o: SpriteOptions = {}): number`: A new sprite (its index), or -1 when full.\n295\t- `set(i: number, o: SpriteOptions & { at?: readonly [number, number, number] }): void`: Move or change sprite `i`.\n296\t- `put(i: number, x: number, y: number, z: number, frame?: number, flip?: boolean): void`: `set` without the options object, for many sprites every frame: position, then frame and mirror.\n297\t- `at(i: number): [number, number, number]`: Where sprite `i` stands.\n298\t- `frameOf(i: number): number`: The frame sprite `i` shows.\n299\t- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n300\t\n301\t### class NavGrid\n302\t\n303\t- `new NavGrid(width: number, height: number, o: NavGridOptions = {})`\n304\t- `static fromRows(rows: readonly string[], o: NavGridOptions & { blocked?: string } = {}): NavGrid`: From an ASCII map: `blocked` characters are closed, everything else open. Default '# ': walls and pits (a ' ' has no floor in RoomKit's maps); past the end of\n305\t- `readonly width: number`\n306\t- `readonly height: number`\n307\t- `readonly cell: number`\n308\t- `readonly ox: number`: World x, z of cell (0, 0)'s corner.\n309\t- `readonly oz: number`\n310\t- `readonly open: Uint8Array`: 1 = walkable.\n311\t- `version: number`: Bumped by `setOpen`: flow fields rebuild.\n312\t- `flowBuilds: number`: How many times a flow field was built (tools: should be one per target cell change).\n313\t- `col(x: number): number`\n314\t- `row(z: number): number`\n315\t- `centerX(col: number): number`: World x, z of a cell's centre.\n316\t- `centerZ(row: number): number`\n317\t- `isOpen(col: number, row: number): boolean`\n318\t- `walkable(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is the world point on a walkable cell?\n319\t- `setOpen(col: number, row: number, open: boolean): void`\n320\t- `path(from: readonly [number, number, number], to: readonly [number, number, number]): [number, number, number][] | null`: Shortest path between two world points (x, z used; y kept from `from`): A* over cells, no corner cutting, then smoothed so it only turn\n321\t- `smooth(pts: [number, number, number][]): [number, number, number][]`: Drop every point the path can skip in a straight line.\n322\t- `lineOfSight(ax: number, az: number, bx: number, bz: number, radius = 0): boolean`: Can you see (or walk straight) from a to b? Walks the cells under the segment (a DDA); `radius` > 0 also tests two lines offset sideways (a body that wide fits).\n323\t- `castWall(ax: number, az: number, dx: number, dz: number, max: number): number`: Distance from a along a direction to the first closed cell (at most `max`): the same cell walk as `lineOfSight`, so a vision cone drawn with it shows exactly what a guard can se\n324\t- `flowTo(x: number, z: number): boolean`: A flow field toward the world point (x, z): the walking distance from every cell to it. Rebuilt only when the target changes cell or the grid changed (`version`). Returns whether it was rebuilt.\n325\t- `flowDistance(x: number, z: number): number`: Walking distance to the flow target from a world point (Infinity: no way).\n326\t- `flowDirection(x: number, z: number, out: [number, number]): boolean`: Which way to go from (x, z) toward the flow target: the unit direction to the lowest-distance neighbour (into `out` [x, z]). Returns false at the target or with no way.\n327\t\n328\t### class WaterSurface\n329\t\n330\t- `new WaterSurface(o: WaterOptions)`\n331\t- `readonly size: readonly [number, number]`\n332\t- `waves: Wave[]`\n333\t- `readonly ripples: RippleField | null`\n334\t- `readonly uOpacity: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n335\t- `readonly uFoamAt: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n336\t- `setWaves(waves: readonly Wave[]): void`: Change the waves (up to MAX_WAVES; the CPU height follows too).\n337\t- `update(time: number, sun?: Vector3): void`: Per frame: the waves' clock (use physics time: floaters read the same), the ripples, the sun.\n338\t- `heightAt(x: number, z: number, t = this.time): number`: World height of the drawn surface at (x, z) (waves and ripples), at the last `update` time or `t`.\n339\t- `covers(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is (x, z) over this water?\n340\t- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n341\t\n342\t### class Timeline\n343\t\n344\t- `new Timeline(o: TimelineOptions)`\n345\t- `readonly duration: number`\n346\t- `time: number`: The playhead (s).\n347\t- `playing: boolean`\n348\t- `speed: number`: Playback rate (1: real time, 0.5: slow motion).\n349\t- `ended: boolean`: True once the end has been reached (by playing or skipping).\n350\t- `readonly fired: string[]`: Cue names in the order they fired (tests and tools).\n351\t- `play(): void`\n352\t- `pause(): void`\n353\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Advance by `dt` seconds of game time (times `speed`) while playing, firing the cues passed.\n354\t- `seek(t: number): void`: Move the playhead to `t` without firing the cues in between (scrubbing). Cues before `t` count as done and cues after it will fire again (going back re-arms them). Ignored while skipping (a cue that loops back cannot undo a skip).\n355\t- `skip(): void`: Jump to the end: the cues not yet fired run (except the cosmetic ones), then `onEnd`. Cues after `duration` never fire, played or skipped.\n356\t- `camera(t = this.time): CameraShot | null`: Where the camera is at `t` (default now): on each key at its time, gliding between keys on a smooth curve (Catmull-Rom tangents, so the speed has no jolt at a key; the first and last key of each shot ease in and ou\n357\t- `line(t = this.time): Line | null`: The subtitle showing at `t` (default now), or null.\n358\t- `bars(t = this.time): number`: How far the letterbox bars are in (0..1) at `t` (default now): sliding in at the start, out at the end.\n359\t\n360\t### interface TimelineOptions\n361\t\n362\t- `shots?: readonly CameraKey[]`\n363\t- `cues?: readonly Cue[]`\n364\t- `lines?: readonly Line[]`\n365\t- `duration?: number`: Length (s); default the last key, cue or line.\n366\t- `bars?: number`: Seconds the letterbox bars take to slide in and out (default 0.6; 0: no bars).\n367\t- `onEnd?: () => void`: Called once when the playhead reaches the end (or the scene is skipped).\n368\t\n369\t### applySky()\n370\t\n371\t```ts\n372\tfunction applySky(engine: Pick<Engine, 'sun' | 'ambient' | 'scene' | 'setSunDirection'>, s: SkyState): void\n373\t```\n374\t\n375\tPut a sky on the engine: sun direction, colour and strength, ambient light, background.\n376\t\n377\t### groundFog()\n378\t\n379\t```ts\n380\tfunction groundFog(o: { color?: number; top?: number; amount?: number; near?: number; far?: number } = {}): { node: Node; color: UniformNode<\"color\", Color>; amount: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; top: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; }\n381\t```\n382\t\n383\tFog for `scene.fogNode` with live settings (uniforms: changing them never recompiles):\n384\tground mist that thickens below `top` (m) and a haze that grows with distance from the\n385\tcamera, both up to `amount` (0..1), in `color` (match the sky).\n386\t\n387\t  const mist = groundFog({ color: s.sky, top: 1.5 });\n388\t  (scene as { fogNode: unknown }).fogNode = mist.node;\n389\t  mist.amount.value = 0.8; mist.color.value.setHex(s.sky);\n390\t\n391\t### class Rng\n392\t\n393\tSeeded randomness. Everything random in a game comes from an `Rng`, so the same seed always\n394\tgives the same result (docs/DOCTRINE.md, principle 1). Each level gets one, `ctx.random`,\n395\tseeded from the engine's seed and the game's name; fork it by purpose so streams stay\n396\tindependent of how many numbers each one drew:\n397\t\n398\t  const level = ctx.random.fork(`level:${n}`);\n399\t  const room = level.fork(`room:${i}`);   // independent of how many numbers `level` drew\n400\t\n401\tNever use Math.random in game or engine code (the lint refuses it).\n402\t\n403\t- `new Rng(seed: number | string)`\n404\t- `get state: number`: The generator's whole state: save it, and set it back to draw the same numbers again.\n405\t- `next(): number`: Uniform in [0, 1). mulberry32.\n406\t- `fork(purpose: string | number): Rng`: A child generator for one purpose; does not advance this one.\n407\t- `range(min: number, max: number): number`: Float in [min, max).\n408\t- `int(min: number, max: number): number`: Integer in [min, max] (inclusive).\n409\t- `chance(p: number): boolean`\n410\t- `pick(items: readonly T[]): T`\n411\t- `weighted(items: readonly T[], weight: (item: T) => number): T`: Pick by weight; entries with weight <= 0 never come up.\n412\t- `shuffle(items: T[]): T[]`\n413\t- `gaussian(mean = 0, sd = 1): number`: Normal distribution (Box–Muller).\n414\t\n415\t### class WindUniforms\n416\t\n417\t- `readonly time: any`\n418\t- `readonly wind: any`\n419\t- `update(time: number, wind: Vector2 | readonly [number, number]): void`: Per frame (game time); `wind` along x and z (its length is the strength).\n420\t\n421\t### swayObject()\n422\t\n423\t```ts\n424\tfunction swayObject(root: Object3D, wind: WindUniforms, o: SwayOptions): void\n425\t```\n426\t\n427\tMake every mesh under `root` sway (its materials replaced by swaying copies).\n428\t\n429\t### class Precipitation\n430\t\n431\t- `new Precipitation(o: PrecipitationOptions)`\n432\t- `readonly kind: PrecipitationKind`\n433\t- `readonly area: readonly [number, number, number]`\n434\t- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind (m/s along x and z): rain slants, snow drifts.\n435\t- `get intensity: number`: Share of the drops falling (0..1).\n436\t- `get speed: number`: Fall speed (m/s).\n437\t- `update(dt: number, center: Vector3): void`: Per frame (game time): advance the fall and follow `center`.\n438\t- `splashes(dt: number): number`: How many drops hit the ground in `dt` seconds at this intensity (for splash particles, ripples).\n439\t- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n440\t\n441\t### class Decals\n442\t\n443\t- `new Decals(scene: Scene, o: { capacity?: number } = {})`\n444\t- `readonly meshes: Map<\"ring\" | \"crack\" | \"footprint\" | \"scorch\" | \"splat\", InstancedMesh<BufferGeometry<NormalBufferAttributes, BufferGeometryEventMap>, Material<...> | Material…`\n445\t- `readonly o: { capacity?: number }`\n446\t- `add(shape: DecalShape, at: Vector3Like, normal: Vector3Like, o: DecalOptions = {}): number`: Lay a mark at `at` on a surface facing `normal`. Returns its index in that shape's pool.\n447\t- `count(shape?: DecalShape): number`: Marks alive (per shape or all).\n448\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Per frame: age the marks; old ones dither away over a second.\n449\t- `clear(): void`: Remove every mark (the meshes stay for reuse).\n450\t- `dispose(): void`\n451\t\n452\t### class Trail\n453\t\n454\t- `new Trail(o: TrailOptions = {})`\n455\t- `readonly capacity: number`\n456\t- `width: number`\n457\t- `life: number`\n458\t- `readonly head: Color`: Head and tail colours (live: `trail.head.setHex(...)`).\n459\t- `readonly tail: Color`\n460\t- `push(p: Vector3, time: number): void`: The trail's newest point (call every frame while it moves).\n461\t- `reset(): void`: Drop every point.\n462\t- `get length: number`: Points alive now.\n463\t- `update(time: number, camera: Camera): void`: Per frame: forget old points, rebuild the ribbon facing `camera`.\n464\t- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n465\t\n466\t### interface LightRequestOptions\n467\t\n468\t- `position?: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]`: World position (copied). Ignored while `follow` is set (then it is `follow` + `offset`).\n469\t- `follow?: Object3D | null`: Follow an object's world position every frame (a projectile, a monster, the hero).\n470\t- `offset?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Offset from `follow` (world axes).\n471\t- `color?: ColorRepresentation`\n472\t- `intensity?: number`: Point light intensity at full strength (before flicker and fades). Default 4.\n473\t- `radius?: number`: Reach in metres (PointLight.distance: light is 0 beyond it). Default 6.\n474\t- `decay?: number`: Distance falloff exponent (PointLight.decay; a uniform, free to change). Default 1.2.\n475\t- `flicker?: FlickerPreset`\n476\t- `priority?: number`: Importance multiplier: the hero's light 4, a loot beam 2, a torch 1 (default).\n477\t- `fadeIn?: number`: Fade times in seconds (defaults 0.25 in, 0.2 out).\n478\t- `fadeOut?: number`\n479\t- `lifetime?: number`: Release automatically after this many seconds (spell flashes, explosions).\n480\t- `name?: string`: Optional label for debugging (`pool.describe()`).\n481\t\n482\t### class BehaviorTree\n483\t\n484\t- `new BehaviorTree(root: BtNode<B>, blackboard: B)`\n485\t- `ticks: number`: Ticks so far.\n486\t- `time: number`: Seconds ticked so far (the sum of every dt).\n487\t- `status: Status | null`\n488\t- `readonly root: BtNode<B>`\n489\t- `readonly blackboard: B`\n490\t- `tick(dt: number): Status`\n491\t- `halt(): void`: Stop everything (a cutscene, a stun): running actions are told to halt.\n492\t- `trace(): TraceRow[]`: Every node depth-first with this tick's answer: for drawing the tree.\n493\t- `activePath(): string[]`: The names down the branch that decided this tick, root first (the last child each node ran that didn't fail).\n494\t\n495\t### bt\n496\t\n497\t```ts\n498\tconst bt: { readonly selector: <B>(name: string, ...children: BtNode<B>[]) => BtNode<B>; readonly sequence: <B>(name: string, ...children: BtNode<B>[]) => BtNode<B>; ... 8 more ...; readonly cooldown: <B>(child: BtNode<...>, seconds: number, name?: string) => \n499\t```\n500\t\n501\tThe builders in one object, for `bt.selector(...)` without importing each.\n502\t\n503\t### compileClips()\n504\t\n505\t```ts\n506\tfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n507\t```\n508\t\n509\t### interface ClipDef\n510\t\n511\t- `name: string`\n512\t- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n513\t- `loop?: boolean`\n514\t- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n515\t- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n516\t- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instea\n517\t- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n518\t- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n519\t- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n520\t- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n521\t- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n522\t- `notes?: string`\n523\t\n524\t### type Key\n525\t\n526\t```ts\n527\ttype Key = readonly [number, Pose, Ease?]\n528\t```\n529\t\n530\t`[frame, pose, easeToNextKey?]`\n531\t\n532\t### gaitClip()\n533\t\n534\t```ts\n535\tfunction gaitClip(rig: RigSpec, g: GaitSpec): ClipDef\n536\t```\n537\t\n538\t### interface GaitSpec\n539\t\n540\tProcedural locomotion cycles with planted feet. Instead of posing every frame, describe\n541\tthe gait (speed, cadence, stance, bounce, arm swing...) and the generator plants each\n542\tfoot on the ground, sliding it backwards at exactly `speed` while it is in stance, then\n543\tswings it forward on an arc — heel strike, roll, toe-off included. Tweak numbers,\n544\tre-run `npm run anim -- check Walk`, look at the sheet.\n545\t\n546\tPhase: the RIGHT heel strikes at frame 0, the left at frames / 2.\n547\t\n548\t- `name: string`\n549\t- `frames: number`: One full cycle (two steps), frames at 30 fps.\n550\t- `speed: number`: Ground speed the cycle is authored for (m/s at playback rate 1).\n551\t- `stance: number`: Fraction of the cycle each foot is on the ground. Walk ≈ 0.55–0.6, run ≈ 0.3.\n552\t- `hip: number`: Mean pelvis height offset from rest (m); negative bends the knees.\n553\t- `bob?: number`: Pelvis bounce amplitude (m), twice per cycle.\n554\t- `low?: number`: Cycle fraction at which the pelvis is lowest (default: walk just after contact, run mid-stance).\n555\t- `squash?: number`: Squash at the lowest point (e.g. 0.05 = 5 % shorter, wider).\n556\t- `lift?: number`: How high the lowest point of the swinging foot clears the floor at its peak (m).\n557\t- `liftPeak?: number`: Where in the swing (0..1) the foot is highest (default 0.4).\n558\t- `reach?: number`: Shift the whole stride forward (m).\n559\t- `swingReach?: number`: Extra forward throw of the swinging foot late in the swing, before it settles (m).\n560\t- `swingDelay?: number`: Swing timing: 0 = even, + = the foot hangs back then whips forward (running).\n561\t- `plant?: number`: 0..1: how much the swinging foot sweeps back to meet the ground at touchdown. At 1 it arrives moving backwards at exactly the ground speed (still, in the world), so the foot plants without sliding; at 0 it eases to a stop relative to the hi\n562\t- `heelStrike?: number`: Toes-up angle at heel strike (deg).\n563\t- `toeOff?: number`: Heel-raise angle at toe-off (deg).\n564\t- `roll?: [heel: number, toe: number]`: Fraction of stance spent rolling off the heel / onto the toes.\n565\t- `tiptoe?: number`: Walk on the balls of the feet: extra pitch through stance (deg), pivoting on the ball.\n566\t- `swingPitch?: number`: Extra toes-down droop mid-swing (deg).\n567\t- `dangle?: number`: How much the swinging foot hangs from the shin mid-swing (0..1, default 0.8).\n568\t- `lean?: number`: Torso lean forward (deg) and twist of the shoulders against the legs (deg).\n569\t- `twist?: number`\n570\t- `sway?: number`: Side-to-side torso roll (deg).\n571\t- `pelvisPitch?: number`: Pelvis pitch (deg): tips the whole body, legs are re-solved.\n572\t- `head?: number`: Head pitch (deg) and how much it counters the torso twist (0..1).\n573\t- `headSteady?: number`\n574\t- `arms?: { /** Swing amplitude (deg). */ swing: number; /** Base elbow bend (deg). */ elbow: number; /** Extra elbow bend when the arm is forward (deg). */ pump?: numbe…`\n575\t- `pose?: Pose`: Extra joints/overrides mixed into every frame (hands, hat...).\n576\t- `extra?: (u: number, pose: Record<string, Euler | JointPose>) => void`: Per-frame hook for anything else: receives the cycle phase 0..1.\n577\t- `step?: number`: Key spacing in frames (default 0.5 — IK is non-linear, keep it fine).\n578\t- `notes?: string`\n579\t\n580\t### HERO_RIG\n581\t\n582\t```ts\n583\tconst HERO_RIG: RigSpec\n584\t```\n585\t\n586\tThe hero rig (public/assets/hero.glb, built by scripts/assets/hero.mjs).\n587\t\n588\tThe character faces +Z. L/R are the character's own sides (its right = -X).\n589\tRotations are Euler XYZ degrees relative to rest; Z is applied first, then Y, then X,\n590\tall about the parent's axes. Verified against contact sheets (`npm run anim -- sheet`).\n591\t\n592\t  Pelvis   +X tip forward (90 = face down, -90 = on its back, 360 = front flip)\n593\t           +Y turn to its left, +Z roll to its RIGHT. p = [x, y, z] offset (m), s = squash\n594\t  Torso    +X bend forward, +Y twist left, +Z lean right\n595\t  Head     +X look down, +Y look left, +Z tilt right\n596\t  Arm*     X swings: -90 = straight ahead, -180 = overhead, +40 = back\n597\t           Z spreads (…\n598\t\n599\t### HERO_RAGDOLL\n600\t\n601\t```ts\n602\tconst HERO_RAGDOLL: readonly RagdollPart[]\n603\t```\n604\t\n605\tThe hero as a ragdoll (the joints of `hero.glb`, see rig.ts): pelvis, torso and head, upper\n606\tarms and legs on ball joints held in cones, elbows and knees as hinges bending the way the\n607\tclips bend them (Forearm −X, Shin +X). Hands and feet ride on their forearm and shin.\n608\t\n609\t### HERO_CLIPS\n610\t\n611\t```ts\n612\tconst HERO_CLIPS: readonly ClipDef[]\n613\t```\n614\t\n615\t### class ScreenFx\n616\t\n617\t- `cover(kind: TransitionKind = 'fade', o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover the picture (progress → 1). Resolves when it stops: `true` when it is fully covered, `false` when another transition (or `set`, `clear`) took over first.\n618\t- `reveal(kind?: TransitionKind, o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Uncover it (progress → 0) with `kind` (default: the one that covered it). Resolves like `cover`.\n619\t- `transition(kind: TransitionKind, between: () => unknown, o: TransitionOptions & { revealCenter?: TransitionOptions['center'] } = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover, run `between` (load a level, teleport), then reveal, also when `between` throws (the error is pas\n620\t- `set(kind: TransitionKind | null, progress: number): void`: Jump straight to a progress (0 clear, 1 covered), no animation.\n621\t- `flash(color: ScreenColor = 'white', o: FlashOptions = {}): void`: A full-screen flash that fades out.\n622\t- `shockwave(at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number], o: ShockwaveOptions = {}): void`: A ring that pushes the picture outwards from `at` (oldest ring is replaced when 4 run).\n623\t- `clear(): void`: Stop everything (a clear picture).\n624\t- `state(): ScreenFxState`\n625\t- `update(dt: number, camera: Camera | null): void`: Engine, every rendered frame (real seconds; `camera` projects world centres).\n626\t- `sampleNode(tex: any, scale: any): any`: Sample an art-resolution picture for the device pass, pushed by the shockwaves and blocked up by a mosaic transition, with the rings' bright edge. `tex` is the art target (a texture node); `scale` is device pixels per\n627\t- `colorNode(color: any, scale: any): any`: The transition and the flash over a finished colour (device pass).\n628\t\n629\t### class Boids\n630\t\n631\t- `new Boids(count: number, o: BoidOptions = {})`\n632\t- `readonly count: number`\n633\t- `readonly pos: Float32Array`\n634\t- `readonly vel: Float32Array`\n635\t- `o: Required<Omit<BoidOptions, 'bounds' | 'seed'>> & { bounds: BoidOptions['bounds'] }`\n636\t- `seek: Vec3 | null`: A point everyone is drawn toward (null: none).\n637\t- `seekWeight: number`\n638\t- `flee: { at: Vec3; radius: number } | null`: A threat to scatter from.\n639\t- `fleeWeight: number`\n640\t- `obstacles: { x: number; y: number; z: number; r: number }[]`: Spheres to steer around (x, y, z, r).\n641\t- `spacing(): number`: Average distance to the nearest neighbour (tests, tools).\n642\t- `order(): { speed: number; alignment: number }`: Average speed and how lined up the headings are (0 random, 1 all the same way).\n643\t- `step(dt: number): void`\n644\t\n645\t### interface BoidOptions\n646\t\n647\t- `bounds?: { min: Vec3; max: Vec3 }`: Agents stay inside this box (they steer back in near its walls).\n648\t- `view?: number`: Neighbour radius (m). Default 2.\n649\t- `space?: number`: Desired gap (m): closer neighbours push apart. Default 0.8.\n650\t- `maxSpeed?: number`\n651\t- `minSpeed?: number`\n652\t- `maxForce?: number`: Steering acceleration limit (m/s²).\n653\t- `separation?: number`: Rule weights.\n654\t- `alignment?: number`\n655\t- `cohesion?: number`\n656\t- `flat?: boolean`: Keep to a horizontal plane (fish in a pond, sheep on a field): y never changes.\n657\t- `margin?: number`: How far inside the bounds the push back in starts (m). Default the view radius; small for a tight pen.\n658\t- `seed?: number`\n659\t\n660\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98060110-4ead-4574-964f-daef0a492581","uuid":"893a9170-b51b-4243-a046-5d9a5412851c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:18.619Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-162030--x07393c-work/98060110-4ead-4574-964f-daef0a492581/tool-results/b1cmd4scw.txt","content":"### class Input\n\nKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n  a frame is not lost.\n- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n\n- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it n\n- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everyt\n- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped,\n- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. \n- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the \n- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n\n### class AudioManager\n\nWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\nsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\npersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n\nBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\nfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\nwas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n\n- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n- `context: AudioContext | null`\n- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n- `get muted: boolean`\n- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Aud\n- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n- `stopMusic(): void`\n- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n\n### interface SoundDef\n\n- `wave: Wave`\n- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n- `sustain?: number`\n- `decay?: number`\n- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n- `arpRate?: number`\n- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n\n### interface Song\n\nA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\nwhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\nEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\nnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n\n- `bpm: number`\n- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n\n### interface Voice\n\nA looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall.\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>`: The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them).\n- `readonly sounding: boolean`: Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started.\n- `readonly stopped: boolean`\n- `set(s: VoiceSettings): void`: Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks).\n- `stop(): void`\n\n### interface VoiceSettings\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 (default 1).\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n- `muffle?: number`: 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones.\n\n### spatialize()\n\n```ts\nfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n```\n\nGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n\n### class Hud\n\nPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\nthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\nlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n\nRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\nresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n\n  ctx.hud.clear();\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n\n- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n- `visible: boolean`\n- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n- `get height: number`\n- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### class Particles\n\nPixel-art particles: CPU-simulated pools, drawn as camera-facing square sprites in flat\npalette colors. One emitter (one instanced draw call, one pooled instance buffer) per\npreset; sizes are whole art pixels, so in Pixel mode a particle is a crisp N×N block.\nAvailable as `ctx.particles`; emitters are created on first use and freed by `clear()`\n(the engine calls it when a level unloads).\n\n  ctx.particles.burst('dust', hero.feet);\n  ctx.particles.burst('skid', feet, { direction: [-vx, 0.3, -vz], count: 2 });\n\n- `new Particles(view: () => ParticleView)`\n- `readonly group: Group<Object3DEventMap>`: Engine-owned group holding every emitter.\n- `readonly presets: Map<string, ParticlePreset>`: Preset registry: the built-ins plus anything registered.\n- `readonly pixelWorld: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`: World units per art pixel at the camera focus (updated every frame).\n- `register(name: string, preset: ParticlePreset): void`: Add or replace a named preset. Replacing frees the old preset's emitter.\n- `burst(preset: string | ParticlePreset, at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number], options: BurstOptions = {}): number`: Spawn a burst of `preset` (a name or a ParticlePreset) at `at`. Returns the number of particles spawned (fewer when the pool is ful\n- `get alive: number`: Particles alive right now, over all emitters.\n- `get emitterCount: number`: Emitters (= draw calls at most) currently allocated.\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Simulate and upload. Called by the engine every frame (frozen while paused).\n- `reseed(rng: Rng): void`: Take bursts' randomness from `rng` (the engine passes each level's `ctx.random` fork).\n- `clear(): void`: Kill every particle and free the emitters (GPU buffers, materials).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### interface ParticlePreset\n\nA particle effect as data. Sizes are in art pixels (whole pixels, so particles stay crisp\nsquares), colors are palette names stepped over each particle's life (no blending).\n\n- `count: Range`: Particles per burst.\n- `life: Range`: Seconds each particle lives.\n- `speed: Range`: Launch speed, world units / s.\n- `direction?: Vec3`: Main launch direction (default straight up).\n- `spread?: number`: Cone half-angle around `direction`, degrees (180 = every direction). Default 180.\n- `flatten?: number`: Multiply vertical launch speed (0.2 = dust that hugs the ground). Default 1.\n- `gravity?: number`: Downward acceleration, world units / s² (negative floats up). Default 0.\n- `drag?: number`: Velocity damping per second (0 = none).\n- `size: readonly [number, number]`: Size in art pixels at birth and at death (rounded to whole pixels).\n- `colors: readonly PaletteColor[]`: Palette colors stepped through over the lifetime.\n- `radius?: number`: Random spawn offset radius, world units.\n- `max?: number`: Pool size (max alive at once). Default 256.\n\n### class Terrain\n\n- `new Terrain(o: TerrainOptions = {})`\n- `readonly nx: number`\n- `readonly nz: number`\n- `readonly sizeX: number`\n- `readonly sizeZ: number`\n- `readonly at: readonly [number, number, number]`\n- `readonly heights: Float32Array`: Heights at the grid points, row by row along z: `heights[ix + iz * (nx + 1)]`.\n- `readonly mesh: Mesh`\n- `collider: RAPIER.Collider | null`\n- `get cell: [number, number]`: Metres between grid points along x and z.\n- `pointX(ix: number): number`: World x, z of grid point (ix, iz).\n- `pointZ(iz: number): number`\n- `generate(f: (x: number, z: number) => number): this`: Fill the heights from `f(x, z)` (world x, z; metres above the terrain's origin), then redraw.\n- `heightAt(x: number, z: number): number`: Height of the surface at world (x, z), in world y: the triangle it is over, as the mesh and the collider have it. Outside the grid: its nearest edge.\n- `normalAt(x: number, z: number, out = new Vector3()): Vector3`: The surface's normal at world (x, z) (central differences over half a cell).\n- `update(): void`: Redraw the mesh from the heights (positions, flat normals, colours per face).\n- `attach(physics: Physics): RAPIER.Collider`: Make (or remake, after `generate` / `erode`) the heightfield collider: a fixed body at the terrain's origin. Returns it.\n- `detach(): void`: Remove the collider (the mesh stays).\n- `dispose(): void`: The collider and the mesh's geometry go (the shared material stays).\n- `erode(o: ErosionOptions = {}): void`: Hydraulic erosion: `droplets` raindrops, each rolling downhill from a random point. A drop carries soil up to a capacity that grows with its speed, water and how steeply it is falling: below capacity it digs (over a sma\n\n### interface TerrainOptions\n\n- `size?: readonly [number, number]`: Size in metres along x and z (default [32, 32]).\n- `cells?: readonly [number, number]`: Cells along x and z (default one per metre).\n- `at?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Where the middle of the grid's height 0 is (default the origin).\n- `bands?: readonly TerrainBand[]`: Colour by height, lowest first; the last band takes everything above.\n- `steep?: number`: Faces steeper than this (their normal's y below it) are rock. Default 0.72 (about 44°).\n- `rock?: number`\n\n### TERRAIN_BANDS\n\n```ts\nconst TERRAIN_BANDS: readonly TerrainBand[]\n```\n\nSea, beach, grass, forest, rock, snow (the engine palette).\n\n### class GrassField\n\n- `new GrassField(o: GrassOptions)`\n- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind along x and z (its length is the strength).\n- `sway: number`: How far blades lean per unit of wind (m at the tip). Default 0.12.\n- `readonly blades: number`: Blades actually placed (the mask can leave fewer than `count`).\n- `push(i: number, at: Vector3Like | null, radius = 0.8): void`: Pusher `i` (0..3): blades within `radius` of `at` part and flatten (radius 0: none).\n- `update(time: number): void`: Per frame (game time).\n- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n\n### interface GrassOptions\n\n- `area: readonly [number, number]`: Width (x) and depth (z) of the patch, m.\n- `at: readonly [number, number, number]`: Centre of the patch on the ground.\n- `count: number`\n- `height?: readonly [number, number]`: Blade heights (m): shortest and tallest. Default [0.25, 0.55].\n- `width?: number`: Blade width at the root (m). Default 0.07.\n- `base?: number`: Colours (hex): roots and tips.\n- `tip?: number`\n- `mask?: (x: number, z: number) => boolean`: Where blades grow (world x, z); default everywhere in the patch.\n- `ground?: (x: number, z: number) => number`: Ground height under a blade (default: the patch's y).\n- `seed?: number`\n\n### class SpriteBatch\n\n- `new SpriteBatch(sheet: SpriteSheet, o: { capacity: number })`\n- `readonly sheet: SpriteSheet`\n- `spawn(at: readonly [number, number, number], o: SpriteOptions = {}): number`: A new sprite (its index), or -1 when full.\n- `set(i: number, o: SpriteOptions & { at?: readonly [number, number, number] }): void`: Move or change sprite `i`.\n- `put(i: number, x: number, y: number, z: number, frame?: number, flip?: boolean): void`: `set` without the options object, for many sprites every frame: position, then frame and mirror.\n- `at(i: number): [number, number, number]`: Where sprite `i` stands.\n- `frameOf(i: number): number`: The frame sprite `i` shows.\n- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n\n### class NavGrid\n\n- `new NavGrid(width: number, height: number, o: NavGridOptions = {})`\n- `static fromRows(rows: readonly string[], o: NavGridOptions & { blocked?: string } = {}): NavGrid`: From an ASCII map: `blocked` characters are closed, everything else open. Default '# ': walls and pits (a ' ' has no floor in RoomKit's maps); past the end of\n- `readonly width: number`\n- `readonly height: number`\n- `readonly cell: number`\n- `readonly ox: number`: World x, z of cell (0, 0)'s corner.\n- `readonly oz: number`\n- `readonly open: Uint8Array`: 1 = walkable.\n- `version: number`: Bumped by `setOpen`: flow fields rebuild.\n- `flowBuilds: number`: How many times a flow field was built (tools: should be one per target cell change).\n- `col(x: number): number`\n- `row(z: number): number`\n- `centerX(col: number): number`: World x, z of a cell's centre.\n- `centerZ(row: number): number`\n- `isOpen(col: number, row: number): boolean`\n- `walkable(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is the world point on a walkable cell?\n- `setOpen(col: number, row: number, open: boolean): void`\n- `path(from: readonly [number, number, number], to: readonly [number, number, number]): [number, number, number][] | null`: Shortest path between two world points (x, z used; y kept from `from`): A* over cells, no corner cutting, then smoothed so it only turn\n- `smooth(pts: [number, number, number][]): [number, number, number][]`: Drop every point the path can skip in a straight line.\n- `lineOfSight(ax: number, az: number, bx: number, bz: number, radius = 0): boolean`: Can you see (or walk straight) from a to b? Walks the cells under the segment (a DDA); `radius` > 0 also tests two lines offset sideways (a body that wide fits).\n- `castWall(ax: number, az: number, dx: number, dz: number, max: number): number`: Distance from a along a direction to the first closed cell (at most `max`): the same cell walk as `lineOfSight`, so a vision cone drawn with it shows exactly what a guard can se\n- `flowTo(x: number, z: number): boolean`: A flow field toward the world point (x, z): the walking distance from every cell to it. Rebuilt only when the target changes cell or the grid changed (`version`). Returns whether it was rebuilt.\n- `flowDistance(x: number, z: number): number`: Walking distance to the flow target from a world point (Infinity: no way).\n- `flowDirection(x: number, z: number, out: [number, number]): boolean`: Which way to go from (x, z) toward the flow target: the unit direction to the lowest-distance neighbour (into `out` [x, z]). Returns false at the target or with no way.\n\n### class WaterSurface\n\n- `new WaterSurface(o: WaterOptions)`\n- `readonly size: readonly [number, number]`\n- `waves: Wave[]`\n- `readonly ripples: RippleField | null`\n- `readonly uOpacity: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n- `readonly uFoamAt: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n- `setWaves(waves: readonly Wave[]): void`: Change the waves (up to MAX_WAVES; the CPU height follows too).\n- `update(time: number, sun?: Vector3): void`: Per frame: the waves' clock (use physics time: floaters read the same), the ripples, the sun.\n- `heightAt(x: number, z: number, t = this.time): number`: World height of the drawn surface at (x, z) (waves and ripples), at the last `update` time or `t`.\n- `covers(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is (x, z) over this water?\n- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n\n### class Timeline\n\n- `new Timeline(o: TimelineOptions)`\n- `readonly duration: number`\n- `time: number`: The playhead (s).\n- `playing: boolean`\n- `speed: number`: Playback rate (1: real time, 0.5: slow motion).\n- `ended: boolean`: True once the end has been reached (by playing or skipping).\n- `readonly fired: string[]`: Cue names in the order they fired (tests and tools).\n- `play(): void`\n- `pause(): void`\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Advance by `dt` seconds of game time (times `speed`) while playing, firing the cues passed.\n- `seek(t: number): void`: Move the playhead to `t` without firing the cues in between (scrubbing). Cues before `t` count as done and cues after it will fire again (going back re-arms them). Ignored while skipping (a cue that loops back cannot undo a skip).\n- `skip(): void`: Jump to the end: the cues not yet fired run (except the cosmetic ones), then `onEnd`. Cues after `duration` never fire, played or skipped.\n- `camera(t = this.time): CameraShot | null`: Where the camera is at `t` (default now): on each key at its time, gliding between keys on a smooth curve (Catmull-Rom tangents, so the speed has no jolt at a key; the first and last key of each shot ease in and ou\n- `line(t = this.time): Line | null`: The subtitle showing at `t` (default now), or null.\n- `bars(t = this.time): number`: How far the letterbox bars are in (0..1) at `t` (default now): sliding in at the start, out at the end.\n\n### interface TimelineOptions\n\n- `shots?: readonly CameraKey[]`\n- `cues?: readonly Cue[]`\n- `lines?: readonly Line[]`\n- `duration?: number`: Length (s); default the last key, cue or line.\n- `bars?: number`: Seconds the letterbox bars take to slide in and out (default 0.6; 0: no bars).\n- `onEnd?: () => void`: Called once when the playhead reaches the end (or the scene is skipped).\n\n### applySky()\n\n```ts\nfunction applySky(engine: Pick<Engine, 'sun' | 'ambient' | 'scene' | 'setSunDirection'>, s: SkyState): void\n```\n\nPut a sky on the engine: sun direction, colour and strength, ambient light, background.\n\n### groundFog()\n\n```ts\nfunction groundFog(o: { color?: number; top?: number; amount?: number; near?: number; far?: number } = {}): { node: Node; color: UniformNode<\"color\", Color>; amount: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; top: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; }\n```\n\nFog for `scene.fogNode` with live settings (uniforms: changing them never recompiles):\nground mist that thickens below `top` (m) and a haze that grows with distance from the\ncamera, both up to `amount` (0..1), in `color` (match the sky).\n\n  const mist = groundFog({ color: s.sky, top: 1.5 });\n  (scene as { fogNode: unknown }).fogNode = mist.node;\n  mist.amount.value = 0.8; mist.color.value.setHex(s.sky);\n\n### class Rng\n\nSeeded randomness. Everything random in a game comes from an `Rng`, so the same seed always\ngives the same result (docs/DOCTRINE.md, principle 1). Each level gets one, `ctx.random`,\nseeded from the engine's seed and the game's name; fork it by purpose so streams stay\nindependent of how many numbers each one drew:\n\n  const level = ctx.random.fork(`level:${n}`);\n  const room = level.fork(`room:${i}`);   // independent of how many numbers `level` drew\n\nNever use Math.random in game or engine code (the lint refuses it).\n\n- `new Rng(seed: number | string)`\n- `get state: number`: The generator's whole state: save it, and set it back to draw the same numbers again.\n- `next(): number`: Uniform in [0, 1). mulberry32.\n- `fork(purpose: string | number): Rng`: A child generator for one purpose; does not advance this one.\n- `range(min: number, max: number): number`: Float in [min, max).\n- `int(min: number, max: number): number`: Integer in [min, max] (inclusive).\n- `chance(p: number): boolean`\n- `pick(items: readonly T[]): T`\n- `weighted(items: readonly T[], weight: (item: T) => number): T`: Pick by weight; entries with weight <= 0 never come up.\n- `shuffle(items: T[]): T[]`\n- `gaussian(mean = 0, sd = 1): number`: Normal distribution (Box–Muller).\n\n### class WindUniforms\n\n- `readonly time: any`\n- `readonly wind: any`\n- `update(time: number, wind: Vector2 | readonly [number, number]): void`: Per frame (game time); `wind` along x and z (its length is the strength).\n\n### swayObject()\n\n```ts\nfunction swayObject(root: Object3D, wind: WindUniforms, o: SwayOptions): void\n```\n\nMake every mesh under `root` sway (its materials replaced by swaying copies).\n\n### class Precipitation\n\n- `new Precipitation(o: PrecipitationOptions)`\n- `readonly kind: PrecipitationKind`\n- `readonly area: readonly [number, number, number]`\n- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind (m/s along x and z): rain slants, snow drifts.\n- `get intensity: number`: Share of the drops falling (0..1).\n- `get speed: number`: Fall speed (m/s).\n- `update(dt: number, center: Vector3): void`: Per frame (game time): advance the fall and follow `center`.\n- `splashes(dt: number): number`: How many drops hit the ground in `dt` seconds at this intensity (for splash particles, ripples).\n- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n\n### class Decals\n\n- `new Decals(scene: Scene, o: { capacity?: number } = {})`\n- `readonly meshes: Map<\"ring\" | \"crack\" | \"footprint\" | \"scorch\" | \"splat\", InstancedMesh<BufferGeometry<NormalBufferAttributes, BufferGeometryEventMap>, Material<...> | Material…`\n- `readonly o: { capacity?: number }`\n- `add(shape: DecalShape, at: Vector3Like, normal: Vector3Like, o: DecalOptions = {}): number`: Lay a mark at `at` on a surface facing `normal`. Returns its index in that shape's pool.\n- `count(shape?: DecalShape): number`: Marks alive (per shape or all).\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Per frame: age the marks; old ones dither away over a second.\n- `clear(): void`: Remove every mark (the meshes stay for reuse).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### class Trail\n\n- `new Trail(o: TrailOptions = {})`\n- `readonly capacity: number`\n- `width: number`\n- `life: number`\n- `readonly head: Color`: Head and tail colours (live: `trail.head.setHex(...)`).\n- `readonly tail: Color`\n- `push(p: Vector3, time: number): void`: The trail's newest point (call every frame while it moves).\n- `reset(): void`: Drop every point.\n- `get length: number`: Points alive now.\n- `update(time: number, camera: Camera): void`: Per frame: forget old points, rebuild the ribbon facing `camera`.\n- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n\n### interface LightRequestOptions\n\n- `position?: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]`: World position (copied). Ignored while `follow` is set (then it is `follow` + `offset`).\n- `follow?: Object3D | null`: Follow an object's world position every frame (a projectile, a monster, the hero).\n- `offset?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Offset from `follow` (world axes).\n- `color?: ColorRepresentation`\n- `intensity?: number`: Point light intensity at full strength (before flicker and fades). Default 4.\n- `radius?: number`: Reach in metres (PointLight.distance: light is 0 beyond it). Default 6.\n- `decay?: number`: Distance falloff exponent (PointLight.decay; a uniform, free to change). Default 1.2.\n- `flicker?: FlickerPreset`\n- `priority?: number`: Importance multiplier: the hero's light 4, a loot beam 2, a torch 1 (default).\n- `fadeIn?: number`: Fade times in seconds (defaults 0.25 in, 0.2 out).\n- `fadeOut?: number`\n- `lifetime?: number`: Release automatically after this many seconds (spell flashes, explosions).\n- `name?: string`: Optional label for debugging (`pool.describe()`).\n\n### class BehaviorTree\n\n- `new BehaviorTree(root: BtNode<B>, blackboard: B)`\n- `ticks: number`: Ticks so far.\n- `time: number`: Seconds ticked so far (the sum of every dt).\n- `status: Status | null`\n- `readonly root: BtNode<B>`\n- `readonly blackboard: B`\n- `tick(dt: number): Status`\n- `halt(): void`: Stop everything (a cutscene, a stun): running actions are told to halt.\n- `trace(): TraceRow[]`: Every node depth-first with this tick's answer: for drawing the tree.\n- `activePath(): string[]`: The names down the branch that decided this tick, root first (the last child each node ran that didn't fail).\n\n### bt\n\n```ts\nconst bt: { readonly selector: <B>(name: string, ...children: BtNode<B>[]) => BtNode<B>; readonly sequence: <B>(name: string, ...children: BtNode<B>[]) => BtNode<B>; ... 8 more ...; readonly cooldown: <B>(child: BtNode<...>, seconds: number, name?: string) => \n```\n\nThe builders in one object, for `bt.selector(...)` without importing each.\n\n### compileClips()\n\n```ts\nfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n```\n\n### interface ClipDef\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n- `loop?: boolean`\n- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instea\n- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n- `notes?: string`\n\n### type Key\n\n```ts\ntype Key = readonly [number, Pose, Ease?]\n```\n\n`[frame, pose, easeToNextKey?]`\n\n### gaitClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction gaitClip(rig: RigSpec, g: GaitSpec): ClipDef\n```\n\n### interface GaitSpec\n\nProcedural locomotion cycles with planted feet. Instead of posing every frame, describe\nthe gait (speed, cadence, stance, bounce, arm swing...) and the generator plants each\nfoot on the ground, sliding it backwards at exactly `speed` while it is in stance, then\nswings it forward on an arc — heel strike, roll, toe-off included. Tweak numbers,\nre-run `npm run anim -- check Walk`, look at the sheet.\n\nPhase: the RIGHT heel strikes at frame 0, the left at frames / 2.\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`: One full cycle (two steps), frames at 30 fps.\n- `speed: number`: Ground speed the cycle is authored for (m/s at playback rate 1).\n- `stance: number`: Fraction of the cycle each foot is on the ground. Walk ≈ 0.55–0.6, run ≈ 0.3.\n- `hip: number`: Mean pelvis height offset from rest (m); negative bends the knees.\n- `bob?: number`: Pelvis bounce amplitude (m), twice per cycle.\n- `low?: number`: Cycle fraction at which the pelvis is lowest (default: walk just after contact, run mid-stance).\n- `squash?: number`: Squash at the lowest point (e.g. 0.05 = 5 % shorter, wider).\n- `lift?: number`: How high the lowest point of the swinging foot clears the floor at its peak (m).\n- `liftPeak?: number`: Where in the swing (0..1) the foot is highest (default 0.4).\n- `reach?: number`: Shift the whole stride forward (m).\n- `swingReach?: number`: Extra forward throw of the swinging foot late in the swing, before it settles (m).\n- `swingDelay?: number`: Swing timing: 0 = even, + = the foot hangs back then whips forward (running).\n- `plant?: number`: 0..1: how much the swinging foot sweeps back to meet the ground at touchdown. At 1 it arrives moving backwards at exactly the ground speed (still, in the world), so the foot plants without sliding; at 0 it eases to a stop relative to the hi\n- `heelStrike?: number`: Toes-up angle at heel strike (deg).\n- `toeOff?: number`: Heel-raise angle at toe-off (deg).\n- `roll?: [heel: number, toe: number]`: Fraction of stance spent rolling off the heel / onto the toes.\n- `tiptoe?: number`: Walk on the balls of the feet: extra pitch through stance (deg), pivoting on the ball.\n- `swingPitch?: number`: Extra toes-down droop mid-swing (deg).\n- `dangle?: number`: How much the swinging foot hangs from the shin mid-swing (0..1, default 0.8).\n- `lean?: number`: Torso lean forward (deg) and twist of the shoulders against the legs (deg).\n- `twist?: number`\n- `sway?: number`: Side-to-side torso roll (deg).\n- `pelvisPitch?: number`: Pelvis pitch (deg): tips the whole body, legs are re-solved.\n- `head?: number`: Head pitch (deg) and how much it counters the torso twist (0..1).\n- `headSteady?: number`\n- `arms?: { /** Swing amplitude (deg). */ swing: number; /** Base elbow bend (deg). */ elbow: number; /** Extra elbow bend when the arm is forward (deg). */ pump?: numbe…`\n- `pose?: Pose`: Extra joints/overrides mixed into every frame (hands, hat...).\n- `extra?: (u: number, pose: Record<string, Euler | JointPose>) => void`: Per-frame hook for anything else: receives the cycle phase 0..1.\n- `step?: number`: Key spacing in frames (default 0.5 — IK is non-linear, keep it fine).\n- `notes?: string`\n\n### HERO_RIG\n\n```ts\nconst HERO_RIG: RigSpec\n```\n\nThe hero rig (public/assets/hero.glb, built by scripts/assets/hero.mjs).\n\nThe character faces +Z. L/R are the character's own sides (its right = -X).\nRotations are Euler XYZ degrees relative to rest; Z is applied first, then Y, then X,\nall about the parent's axes. Verified against contact sheets (`npm run anim -- sheet`).\n\n  Pelvis   +X tip forward (90 = face down, -90 = on its back, 360 = front flip)\n           +Y turn to its left, +Z roll to its RIGHT. p = [x, y, z] offset (m), s = squash\n  Torso    +X bend forward, +Y twist left, +Z lean right\n  Head     +X look down, +Y look left, +Z tilt right\n  Arm*     X swings: -90 = straight ahead, -180 = overhead, +40 = back\n           Z spreads (…\n\n### HERO_RAGDOLL\n\n```ts\nconst HERO_RAGDOLL: readonly RagdollPart[]\n```\n\nThe hero as a ragdoll (the joints of `hero.glb`, see rig.ts): pelvis, torso and head, upper\narms and legs on ball joints held in cones, elbows and knees as hinges bending the way the\nclips bend them (Forearm −X, Shin +X). Hands and feet ride on their forearm and shin.\n\n### HERO_CLIPS\n\n```ts\nconst HERO_CLIPS: readonly ClipDef[]\n```\n\n### class ScreenFx\n\n- `cover(kind: TransitionKind = 'fade', o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover the picture (progress → 1). Resolves when it stops: `true` when it is fully covered, `false` when another transition (or `set`, `clear`) took over first.\n- `reveal(kind?: TransitionKind, o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Uncover it (progress → 0) with `kind` (default: the one that covered it). Resolves like `cover`.\n- `transition(kind: TransitionKind, between: () => unknown, o: TransitionOptions & { revealCenter?: TransitionOptions['center'] } = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover, run `between` (load a level, teleport), then reveal, also when `between` throws (the error is pas\n- `set(kind: TransitionKind | null, progress: number): void`: Jump straight to a progress (0 clear, 1 covered), no animation.\n- `flash(color: ScreenColor = 'white', o: FlashOptions = {}): void`: A full-screen flash that fades out.\n- `shockwave(at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number], o: ShockwaveOptions = {}): void`: A ring that pushes the picture outwards from `at` (oldest ring is replaced when 4 run).\n- `clear(): void`: Stop everything (a clear picture).\n- `state(): ScreenFxState`\n- `update(dt: number, camera: Camera | null): void`: Engine, every rendered frame (real seconds; `camera` projects world centres).\n- `sampleNode(tex: any, scale: any): any`: Sample an art-resolution picture for the device pass, pushed by the shockwaves and blocked up by a mosaic transition, with the rings' bright edge. `tex` is the art target (a texture node); `scale` is device pixels per\n- `colorNode(color: any, scale: any): any`: The transition and the flash over a finished colour (device pass).\n\n### class Boids\n\n- `new Boids(count: number, o: BoidOptions = {})`\n- `readonly count: number`\n- `readonly pos: Float32Array`\n- `readonly vel: Float32Array`\n- `o: Required<Omit<BoidOptions, 'bounds' | 'seed'>> & { bounds: BoidOptions['bounds'] }`\n- `seek: Vec3 | null`: A point everyone is drawn toward (null: none).\n- `seekWeight: number`\n- `flee: { at: Vec3; radius: number } | null`: A threat to scatter from.\n- `fleeWeight: number`\n- `obstacles: { x: number; y: number; z: number; r: number }[]`: Spheres to steer around (x, y, z, r).\n- `spacing(): number`: Average distance to the nearest neighbour (tests, tools).\n- `order(): { speed: number; alignment: number }`: Average speed and how lined up the headings are (0 random, 1 all the same way).\n- `step(dt: number): void`\n\n### interface BoidOptions\n\n- `bounds?: { min: Vec3; max: Vec3 }`: Agents stay inside this box (they steer back in near its walls).\n- `view?: number`: Neighbour radius (m). Default 2.\n- `space?: number`: Desired gap (m): closer neighbours push apart. Default 0.8.\n- `maxSpeed?: number`\n- `minSpeed?: number`\n- `maxForce?: number`: Steering acceleration limit (m/s²).\n- `separation?: number`: Rule weights.\n- `alignment?: number`\n- `cohesion?: number`\n- `flat?: boolean`: Keep to a horizontal plane (fish in a pond, sheep on a field): y never changes.\n- `margin?: number`: How far inside the bounds the push back in starts (m). Default the view radius; small for a tight pen.\n- `seed?: number`\n\n","numLines":660,"startLine":1,"totalLines":660}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01869t1sdqsV3dYob3wqtUPn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
