{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011bBdanZCTFLtspgaEe4g3H","type":"tool_result","content":"0 passed, 0 failed, 7 suites unchanged\nengine-ok\n26\ndate : 2026-11-05 16:20:30\nsource : Far Cry 2\nplan : PLAN.md\ngenre : Open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure and systemic fire\nperspective : First person on foot, at the wheel and in scenes; optional chase camera in vehicles\ntech : Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit), three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, WebGPU, JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild 0.28 into one HTML file; AI Hub session client 1.4.0\nartDirection : The engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art at 480x270, toon materials, 16-colour palette; about 90 new models as data, plus library props\nanimation : The engine's ClipDef system, compileClips and AnimationMixer; hero clips reused, new people clips, a hands rig with surgery scenes; Ragdoll on death; procedural sway\naudio : All in code: SoundDef effects, Song music (7 pieces, music 10% by default), Animalese-style gibberish voices, spatial ambience. No audio files\nonline : AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1 to 4 players; on its own: solo with AI mercs and two windows on one computer\ntesting : Headless Node suites, scripted playthrough, Playwright Chromium on file://, a stand-in gamepad, a phone profile, the hub harness; one command; few screenshots\nresearch : None beyond the plan, the engine kit's docs and the hub's README\nenvironment : Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\nmodel : Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\nusage : added on import\ntestingFeedback\n- 25 logged in tests/ISSUES.md, most of them by the scripted playthrough and the systems suite, a few only by looking at screenshots.\n- 14. Among them: no gun visible at all, the windscreen blocking the whole view from the driving seat, a crash when a soldier burned to death inside his own brain tick, the M8 ride never ending, FINISH IT eaten by the melee key, and grass fire eating the entire map.\n- The bot that plays M0 through both endings with real keys. It found step-order, input-conflict and state bugs no unit test would have, and it runs in Node in a couple of minutes.\n- Screenshots of menus: they cost about three minutes each on this shared machine and the state checks had already proved what they showed. Screenshots of the world, on the other hand, were essential.\nengineFeedback\n- Engine.headless. The same game, physics and all, in Node with no GPU, booted in about a second. Models and sounds as checked data came close behind.\n- Guessed wrong on rowOf and around, which take other arguments than I assumed, and on model ids that clash with the library. Took the groundFog setting from the plan: a negative top inverts its smoothstep and tints everything, which I found by reading its source.\n- The fire grid and everything hung on it: terrain scars, flora swaps, grass blades, soldiers panicking, tanks going up, the map in ink. Then the action layer and the mission runner.\n- It is shaped for a platformer. A first-person open world means replacing the rig step, the pad map and the pointer lock logic. And a press queued in a menu stays alive for 150 ms and leaks into play unless you clear it.\n- none\n- A real first-person controller preset with no reserved keys, pad codes kept apart from keyboard codes, and an instanced model helper. And fix groundFog for a top below the camera.\npromptFeedback\n- The plan decided almost everything and said what it had verified, so I could build instead of deciding. Its traps table saved hours. The build order and cut order made the size manageable.\n- The plan calls a line verified that turned out wrong for the look (the fog setting). The kit says modules do not load from file:// while the plan rightly says the bundle does. And the brief asks for file naming by GameTitle and SOURCE without saying how to treat spaces.\n- Keeping one sim working as host, client and headless server at once, and judging the look at a frame every few seconds on a shared machine. I could not play it by hand at speed, so game feel and balance are the least proven part.\n- Allow a real-time playtest budget, or say plainly that feel is judged later. Ask for a smaller world or fewer missions if polish matters more than breadth: this scope spreads effort thin. Keep the plan, the traps table and the one test command.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98060110-4ead-4574-964f-daef0a492581","uuid":"55b9b357-1e2e-4ab8-a068-43f285728f1e","timestamp":"2026-11-06T02:34:14.500Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"0 passed, 0 failed, 7 suites unchanged\nengine-ok\n26\ndate : 2026-11-05 16:20:30\nsource : Far Cry 2\nplan : PLAN.md\ngenre : Open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure and systemic fire\nperspective : First person on foot, at the wheel and in scenes; optional chase camera in vehicles\ntech : Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit), three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, WebGPU, JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild 0.28 into one HTML file; AI Hub session client 1.4.0\nartDirection : The engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art at 480x270, toon materials, 16-colour palette; about 90 new models as data, plus library props\nanimation : The engine's ClipDef system, compileClips and AnimationMixer; hero clips reused, new people clips, a hands rig with surgery scenes; Ragdoll on death; procedural sway\naudio : All in code: SoundDef effects, Song music (7 pieces, music 10% by default), Animalese-style gibberish voices, spatial ambience. No audio files\nonline : AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1 to 4 players; on its own: solo with AI mercs and two windows on one computer\ntesting : Headless Node suites, scripted playthrough, Playwright Chromium on file://, a stand-in gamepad, a phone profile, the hub harness; one command; few screenshots\nresearch : None beyond the plan, the engine kit's docs and the hub's README\nenvironment : Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\nmodel : Anthro