{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgktmJPvfSvhbUTtXZkAe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Far Dry Season** is built and handed in, and everything in `PLAN.md` that can be done on this computer is done. It's an original first-person war-zone shooter: you're hunting the Surveyor across a burning plateau while your guns rust, your trucks die and fever keeps its own timetable.\n\n## Test results\n- **Scripted playthrough:** a bot played the whole story through both endings using real inputs for the objectives, though it teleports between them. One run had a friend dead.\n- **Test suites:** the last full run (`node tests/run.mjs --all`) had one failure, a browser crash while I was rendering screenshots beside it. `TASK.md` says that crash means memory ran out, and the browser suite then passed all 25 checks when run alone.\n- **Multiplayer:** all four AI Hub scenarios pass, and so does a two-window round on one computer.\n- **Engine:** `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the kit.\n\n## What hasn't been proven\n- **Game feel and balance:** nobody has played it at full speed, because software rendering here only manages a frame every few seconds. Balance rests on simulation and the dev sliders. Standing still in the open against one post, you last 20+ seconds at 70 m, about 8 at 40 m and under 3 at 20 m.\n- **Real hardware and sound:** the controller was a stand-in, and no one has listened to the audio. I only checked that every effect and voice renders non-silent.\n- **Weakest visuals:**\n    - The field surgery scenes read as a forearm, a wound and a reaching hand, but they're still blocky, and the pliers were hidden in the frame I checked.\n    - I didn't re-shoot the rifle at night after shrinking it, because that shot was cut off by a timeout.\n    - The last steering-wheel and ending-card fixes also weren't re-shot.\n\nNothing is committed to git.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 16:20:30\n- **Source:** Far Cry 2\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure and systemic fire; first person on foot, at the wheel and in scenes, with an optional chase camera in vehicles\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit), three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, WebGPU, JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild 0.28 into one HTML file; AI Hub session client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art at 480×270, toon materials, 16-colour palette; about 90 new models as data, plus library props\n- **Animation:** the engine's ClipDef system, compileClips and AnimationMixer; hero clips reused, new people clips, a hands rig with surgery scenes; Ragdoll on death; procedural sway\n- **Audio:** all in code: SoundDef effects, Song music (7 pieces, music 10% by default), Animalese-style gibberish voices, spatial ambience; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1 to 4 players; on its own: solo with AI mercs, or two windows on one computer\n- **Testing:** headless Node suites, scripted playthrough, Playwright Chromium on file://, a stand-in gamepad, a phone profile, the hub harness; one command; few screenshots\n- **Research:** none beyond the plan, the engine kit's docs and the hub's README\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 25, logged in `tests/ISSUES.md`. Most came from the scripted playthrough and the systems suite, a few only from looking at screenshots, and four from reading the input, save and net code.\n- **How many were critical or game breaking?** 14. Among them:\n    - no gun visible at all\n    - the windscreen blocking the whole view from the driving seat\n    - a crash when a soldier burned to death in the middle of their own AI update\n    - the M8 train ride never ending\n    - FINISH IT being swallowed by the melee key\n    - grass fire eating the entire map\n- **Most helpful test?** The bot that plays M0 through both endings with real inputs. It found step-order, input-conflict and state bugs no unit test would have.\n- **Least helpful test?** Screenshots of menus. They cost about three minutes each on this shared machine, and state checks had already proved what they showed. Screenshots of the world, though, were essential.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** `Engine.headless`: the same game, physics included, in Node with no GPU, booting in about a second. Models and sounds as checked data came close behind.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** I guessed wrong on `rowOf` and `around`, which take different arguments than I assumed, and on model ids that clash with the library. I also took the `groundFog` setting from the plan; a negative `top` inverts its smoothstep and tints everything, which I found by reading its source.\n- **Most important thing you built yourself?** The fire grid and everything hung on it: terrain scars, flora swaps, grass blades, panicking soldiers, tanks going up, the map in ink. Then the action layer and the mission runner.\n- **Hardest part of using the engine?** It's shaped for a platformer. A first-person open world means replacing the rig step, the pad map and the pointer-lock logic. A press queued in a menu also stays alive for 150 ms and leaks into play unless you clear it.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None.\n- **One thing you'd change?** A real first-person controller preset with no reserved keys, pad codes kept apart from keyboard codes, and an instanced model helper. And fix `groundFog` for a `top` below the camera.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** The plan decided almost everything and said what it had verified, so I could build instead of deciding. Its traps table saved hours, and the build and cut orders made the size manageable.\n- **What was confusing?**\n    - The plan calls one line verified that turned out wrong for the look (the fog setting).\n    - The kit says modules don't load from `file://`, while the plan rightly says the bundle does.\n    - The brief asks for file naming by GameTitle and SOURCE without saying how to treat spaces.\n- **Hardest thing to do?** Keeping one simulation working as host, client and headless server at once, and judging the look at a frame every few seconds. I couldn't play it by hand at speed, so feel and balance are the least proven part.\n- **What would you change?** Allow a real-time playtest budget, or say plainly that feel is judged later. Ask for a smaller world or fewer missions if polish matters more than breadth, because this scope spreads effort thin. Keep the plan, the traps table and the one test command.\n\nFiles are in `work/`:\n- `FarDrySeason-FarCry2-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162030.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `FarDrySeason-FarCry2-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162030/` (dev folder; `DESIGNERS.md` explains how to add content)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2261,"cache_read_input_tokens":777502,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2261},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98060110-4ead-4574-964f-daef0a492581","uuid":"7447b4fd-8bea-4ca0-a68e-9c63a75223e3","timestamp":"2026-11-06T02:34:38.495Z","request_id":"req_011CgktmHXLwjhgSsFYFM8dc"}
