port', `About shows all ${FIELDS.length} Source Information fields, Usage: added on import`);\nconst startsPage = (q) => pages.some((p) => text(p).startsWith(q));\nline(startsPage(ABOUT.testing[0][0]) && startsPage(ABOUT.engine[0][0]) && startsPage(ABOUT.prompt[0][0]), 'Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback each start on their own page');\nline(ABOUT.testing.length === 4 && ABOUT.engine.length === 6 && ABOUT.prompt.length === 4 && [...ABOUT.testing, ...ABOUT.engine, ...ABOUT.prompt].every((r) => r[1]) && [...ABOUT.testing, ...ABOUT.engine, ...ABOUT.prompt].every((r) => pages.some((p) => text(p).includes(r[0]))), \"The brief's questions, in order, each answered and on screen\");\nline(['PIXEL ENGINE (MIT)', 'THREE.JS (MIT)', 'RAPIER (APACHE-2.0)', 'THE AI HUB SESSION CLIENT', 'INSPIRED BY FAR CRY 2; NOT AFFILIATED.'].every((c) => ABOUT.credits.includes(c)) && text(pages.at(-1)).includes('PIXEL ENGINE'), 'Credits: Pixel Engine, three.js, Rapier, the AI Hub client, not affiliated');\nline(existsSync('DESIGNERS.md') && existsSync(html) && existsSync(resolve(root, '..', 'exit-interview.json')), 'Hand-in present: the HTML, exit-interview.json, the dev folder');\n\"\"\"\nopen('tests/lib/checklist.mjs','w').write(s)\nEOF\nnode tests/lib/checklist.mjs 2>&1 | cut -c1-170; node tests/run.mjs | tail -1; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('../exit-interview.json'))\nfor s in ['testingFeedback','engineFeedback','promptFeedback']:\n    print(s); [print('-',x) for x in d[s]]\nEOF","description":"Update audit, extend checklist, print final answers","timeout":60000}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XpHriLb5BwK9n4J31nKFxi","type":"tool_result","content":"PASS  Title in <title> and on the title screen (browser suite passed on this code)\nPASS  One HTML file runs from file:// with nothing beside it (browser suite)\nPASS  --all: every suite last passed on exactly this code: static=ok systems=ok playthrough=ok replay=ok browser=ok hub=ok link=ok\nPASS  All four hub scenarios pass and the game runs outside a session\nPASS  Bot finished both endings\nPASS  No TODOs, placeholders or stubs (static suite scans src/)\nPASS  Every screen: pad PlayStation, pad Xbox, keyboard and mouse prompts (systems suite walks all 17 screens in 3 modes)\nPASS  All ten screenshots the plan lists exist and were reviewed\nPASS  pixel-engine.js byte-identical to the kit\nPASS  About and Credits filled in\nPASS  exit-interview.json parses, right shape, every answer present\nPASS  About shows all 15 Source Information fields, Usage: added on import\nPASS  Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback each start on their own page\nPASS  The brief's questions, in order, each answered and on screen\nFAIL  Credits: Pixel Engine, three.js, Rapier, the AI Hub client, not affiliated\nPASS  Hand-in present: the HTML, exit-interview.json, the dev folder\n0 passed, 0 failed, 7 suites unchanged\ntestingFeedback\n- 33 logged in tests/ISSUES.md, most of them by the scripted playthrough and the systems suite, a few only by looking at screenshots.\n- 16. Among them: no gun visible at all, the windscreen blocking the whole view from the driving seat, a crash when a soldier burned to death inside his own brain tick, the M8 ride never ending, FINISH IT eaten by the melee key, and grass fire eating the entire map.\n- The bot that plays M0 through both endings with real keys. It found step-order, input-conflict and state bugs no unit test would have, and it runs in Node in a couple of minutes.\n- Screenshots of menus: they cost about three minutes each on this shared machine and the state checks had already proved what they showed. Screenshots of the world, on the other hand, were essential.\nengineFeedback\n- Engine.headless. The same game, physics and all, in Node with no GPU, booted in about a second. Models and sounds as checked data came close behind.\n- Guessed wrong on rowOf and around, which take other arguments than I assumed, and on model ids that clash with the library. Took the groundFog setting from the plan: a negative top inverts its smoothstep and tints everything, which I found by reading its source.\n- The fire grid and everything hung on it: terrain scars, flora swaps, grass blades, soldiers panicking, tanks going up, the map in ink. Then the action layer and the mission runner.\n- It is shaped for a platformer. A first-person open world means replacing the rig step, the pad map and the pointer lock logic. And a press queued in a menu stays alive for 150 ms and leaks into play unless you clear it.\n- none\n- A real first-person controller preset with no reserved keys, pad codes kept apart from keyboard codes, and an instanced model helper. And fix groundFog for a top below the camera.\npromptFeedback\n- The plan decided almost everything and said what it had verified, so I could build instead of deciding. Its traps table saved hours. The build order and cut order made the size manageable.\n- The plan calls a line verified that turned out wrong for the look (the fog setting). The kit says modules do not load from file:// while the plan rightly says the bundle does. And the brief asks for file naming by GameTitle and SOURCE without saying how to treat spaces.\n- Keeping one sim working as host, client and headless server at once, and judging the look at a frame every few seconds on a shared machine. I could not play it by hand at speed, so game feel and balance are the least proven part.\n- Allow a real-time playtest budget, or say plainly that feel is judged later. Ask for a smaller world or fewer missions if polish matters more than breadth: this scope spreads effort thin. Keep the plan, the traps table and the one test command.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98060110-4ead-4574-964f-daef0a492581","uuid":"01400c6a-5fc3-4841-92eb-ea8bf627b190","timestamp":"2026-11-06T04:54:14.501Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"PASS  Title in <title> and on the title screen (browser suite passed on this code)\nPASS  One HTML file runs from file:// with nothing beside it (browser suite)\nPASS  --all: every suite last passed on exactly this code: static=ok systems=ok playthrough=ok replay=ok browser=ok hub=ok link=ok\nPASS  All four hub scenarios pass and the game runs outside a session\nPASS  Bot finished both endings\nPASS  No TODOs, placeholders or stubs (static suite scans src/)\nPASS  Every screen: pad PlayStation, pad Xbox, keyboard and mouse prompts (systems suite walks all 17 screens in 3 modes)\nPASS  All ten screenshots the plan lists exist and were reviewed\nPASS  pixel-engine.js byte-identical to the kit\nPASS  About and Credits filled in\nPASS  exit-interview.json parses, right shape, every answer present\nPASS  About shows all 15 Source Information fields, Usage: added on import\nPASS  Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback each start on their own page\nPASS  The brief's questions, in order, each answered and on screen\nFAIL  Credits: Pixel Engine, three.js, Rapier, the AI Hub client, not affiliated\nPASS  Hand-in present: the HTML, exit-interview.json, the dev folder\n0 passed, 0 failed, 7 suites unchanged\ntestingFeedback\n- 33 logged in tests/ISSUES.md, most of them by the scripted playthrough and the systems suite, a few only by looking at screenshots.\n- 16. Among them: no gun visible at all, the windscreen blocking the whole view from the driving seat, a crash when a soldier burned to death inside his own brain tick, the M8 ride never ending, FINISH IT eaten by the melee key, and grass fire eating the entire map.\n- The bot that plays M0 through both endings with real keys. It found step-order, input-conflict and state bugs no unit test would have, and it runs in Node in a couple of minutes.\n- Screenshots of menus: they cost about three minutes each on this shared machine and the state checks had already proved what they showed. Screenshots of the world, on the other hand, were essential.\nengineFeedback\n- Engine.headless. The same game, physics and all, in Node with no GPU, booted in about a second. Models and sounds as checked data came close behind.\n- Guessed wrong on rowOf and around, which take other arguments than I assumed, and on model ids that clash with the library. Took the groundFog setting from the plan: a negative top inverts its smoothstep and tints everything, which I found by reading its source.\n- The fire grid and everything hung on it: terrain scars, flora swaps, grass blades, soldiers panicking, tanks going up, the map in ink. Then the action layer and the mission runner.\n- It is shaped for a platformer. A first-person open world means replacing the rig step, the pad map and the pointer lock logic. And a press queued in a menu stays alive for 150 ms and leaks into play unless you clear it.\n- none\n- A real first-person controller preset with no reserved keys, pad codes kept apart from keyboard codes, and an instanced model helper. And fix groundFog for a top below the camera.\npromptFeedback\n- The plan decided almost everything and said what it had verified, so I could build instead of deciding. Its traps table saved hours. The build order and cut order made the size manageable.\n- The plan calls a line verified that turned out wrong for the look (the fog setting). The kit says modules do not load from file:// while the plan rightly says the bundle does. And the brief asks for file naming by GameTitle and SOURCE without saying how to treat spaces.\n- Keeping one sim working as host, client and headless server at once, and judging the look at a frame every few seconds on a shared machine. I could not play it by hand at speed, so game feel and balance are the least proven part.\n- Allow a real-time playtest budget, or say plainly that feel is judged later. Ask for a smaller world or fewer missions if polish matters more than breadth: this scope spreads effort thin. Keep the plan, the traps table and the one test command.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XpHriLb5BwK9n4J31nKFxi","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
