{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgm6xaJbnsV5X37hFiitz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Far Dry Season** is finished and handed in, and everything in `PLAN.md` that this computer can do is done. On the final build, `node tests/run.mjs --all` reports **2,019 passed, 0 failed**, with all seven suites run, and `node tests/lib/checklist.mjs` passes all 16 lines. Those lines cover `PLAN.md` section 27 plus the hand-in files. The screenshot line can only confirm the files exist; I looked at them myself during this session.\n\n**One serious bug turned up in this last pass:** the About and Credits screen, which the brief marks as critical, was drawing every body line wrongly, and I had never taken a screenshot of it. It's fixed and covered by a test. I reviewed three pages by eye before a final tweak; that tweak reworded the questions to match the brief exactly and keeps each answer on one page, and I checked it by test, not by a new screenshot. `tests/PLAN-AUDIT.md` is now up to date with the final build.\n\n**What this computer can't do:**\n- **Full-speed play:** software rendering here is about a frame a second, and `PLAN.md` says not to judge the game by real time. Game feel and balance still need a person with a GPU, and dev mode's sliders are there for that.\n- **Physical controller and human listening:** `TASK.md` lists both under \"What this computer doesn't have\" and says they are \"never required\", so I used its stand-ins.\n\nNothing is committed to git.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 16:20:30\n- **Source:** Far Cry 2\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure and systemic fire; first person on foot, at the wheel and in scenes, with an optional chase camera in vehicles\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit), three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, WebGPU, JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild 0.28 into one HTML file; AI Hub session client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art at 480×270, toon materials, 16-colour palette; about 90 new models as data, plus library props\n- **Animation:** the engine's ClipDef system, compileClips and AnimationMixer; hero clips reused, new people clips, a hands rig with surgery scenes; Ragdoll on death; procedural sway\n- **Audio:** all in code: SoundDef effects, Song music (7 pieces, music 10% by default), Animalese-style gibberish voices, spatial ambience; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1 to 4 players; on its own: solo with AI mercs, or two windows on one computer\n- **Testing:** headless Node suites, scripted playthrough, Playwright Chromium on file://, a stand-in gamepad, a phone profile, the hub harness; one command; few screenshots\n- **Research:** none beyond the plan, the engine kit's docs and the hub's README\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 34, logged in `tests/ISSUES.md`. Most came from the scripted playthrough and the systems suite, a few only from looking at screenshots.\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 17. Among them: no gun visible at all, the windscreen blocking the whole view from the driving seat, a crash when a soldier burned to death in the middle of their own AI update, the M8 ride never ending, FINISH IT eaten by the melee key, and grass fire eating the entire map.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The bot that plays M0 through both endings with real keys. It found step-order, input-conflict and state bugs no unit test would have, and it runs in Node in a couple of minutes.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Screenshots of menus: they cost about three minutes each on this shared machine, and the state checks had already proved what they showed. Screenshots of the world, on the other hand, were essential. (This answer predates the About bug, which state checks had missed and no menu shot had ever covered.)\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** `Engine.headless`: the same game, physics and all, in Node with no GPU, booted in about a second. Models and sounds as checked data came close behind.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** I guessed wrong on `rowOf` and `around`, which take other arguments than I assumed, and on model ids that clash with the library. I also took the `groundFog` setting from the plan: a negative `top` inverts its smoothstep and tints everything, which I found by reading its source.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The fire grid and everything hung on it: terrain scars, flora swaps, grass blades, soldiers panicking, tanks going up, the map in ink. Then the action layer and the mission runner.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** It is shaped for a platformer. A first-person open world means replacing the rig step, the pad map and the pointer-lock logic. And a press queued in a menu stays alive for 150 ms and leaks into play unless you clear it.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A real first-person controller preset with no reserved keys, pad codes kept apart from keyboard codes, and an instanced model helper. And fix `groundFog` for a `top` below the camera.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The plan decided almost everything and said what it had verified, so I could build instead of deciding. Its traps table saved hours. The build order and cut order made the size manageable.\n- **What was confusing?** The plan calls one line verified that turned out wrong for the look (the fog setting). The kit says modules do not load from `file://`, while the plan rightly says the bundle does. And the brief asks for file naming by GameTitle and SOURCE without saying how to treat spaces.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Keeping one simulation working as host, client and headless server at once, and judging the look at a frame every few seconds on a shared machine. I could not play it by hand at speed, so game feel and balance are the least proven part.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Allow a real-time playtest budget, or say plainly that feel is judged later. Ask for a smaller world or fewer missions if polish matters more than breadth: this scope spreads effort thin. Keep the plan, the traps table and the one test command.\n\nFiles are in `work/`:\n- `FarDrySeason-FarCry2-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162030.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `FarDrySeason-FarCry2-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162030/` (dev folder, with `DESIGNERS.md`, `tests/PLAN-AUDIT.md`, `tests/ISSUES.md` and the screenshots in `tests/shots/`)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1137,"cache_read_input_tokens":948202,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1137},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98060110-4ead-4574-964f-daef0a492581","uuid":"08981431-6ba1-4d30-84b1-9325addd571a","timestamp":"2026-11-06T05:14:28.336Z","request_id":"req_011Cgm6xZrZHR63BiE4dVdQY"}
