{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V8jViqAQGq2eAiAVstL1E8","type":"tool_result","content":"9. Shared-world → ghost → parallel (section 19).\n\nNever cut: fire, wear and jams, fever, the held map, buddy rescue and the finish-it choice, Harrow's twist, dev mode with sliders, attract mode, glyphs and native mouse, touch gate, the hub check, music at 10%, the voices.\n\n---\n\n## 27. Done, reports, coverage\n\n**Done means:** every line below is true and you've checked it.\n- [ ] Your title, chosen by the brief's procedure, appears on the title screen and in `<title>`.\n- [ ] The one HTML file runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it.\n- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs --all` reports 0 failed.\n- [ ] All four hub scenarios pass, and the game still runs outside a session.\n- [ ] You finished the game yourself by bot, both endings.\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features anywhere.\n- [ ] Every screen shows native prompts for pad (PlayStation and Xbox glyphs) and keyboard/mouse.\n- [ ] You looked at the screenshots.\n- [ ] `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the copy in the kit's zip (keep the zip in `vendor/` and compare in the `static` suite).\n\n**About and Credits screen** (in-game, paged, reached from main menu and pause; text in `src/content/about.js`). Your brief marks this as critical. It must show:\n- **Source Information,** with every field your brief lists: Date (from your brief), Source (Far Cry 2), Plan (the file name of this plan as you received it), Genre and Perspective, Tech (engine and its version, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, esbuild, your additi\n- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback:** your brief's questions and your honest answers, in order, each on its own page. Don't estimate cost.\n- **Credits:** Pixel Engine (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), the AI Hub session client, and \"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\".\n\n**Your final chat reply** repeats the same Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback.\n\n**The exit interview file** your brief specifies goes beside the HTML, in the exact JSON shape it gives, every value filled, and must parse. Suggested catalogue values: type `Game`; genre `Shooter`; source `Far Cry 2`; series `Far Cry`; players `1–4`; tags suc\n\n**Where each line of your brief is covered:**\n256:- **`TUNING`.** Every balance number lives in `src/content/tuning-defaults.js`, copied to a live `TUNING` object. Systems read `TUNING.x` at the moment of use and never cache it, so sliders act in\n755:| **Balance** | Live sliders (below), **Reset to Defaults** for all, Square/R resets one |\n758:**Balance sliders** (left/right steps, hold accelerates, mouse drags). Defaults in `src/content/tuning-defaults.js`:\n1055:| 9 Polish | All music, ambience, feel, art passes, balance with the sliders, screenshots | You'd show it to someone |\nPASS  Title in <title> and on the title screen (browser suite passed on this code)\nPASS  One HTML file runs from file:// with nothing beside it (browser suite)\nPASS  --all: every suite last passed on exactly this code: static=ok systems=ok playthrough=ok replay=ok browser=ok hub=\nPASS  All four hub scenarios pass and the game runs outside a session\nPASS  Bot finished both endings\nPASS  No TODOs, placeholders or stubs (static suite scans src/)\nPASS  Every screen: pad PlayStation, pad Xbox, keyboard and mouse prompts (systems suite walks all 17 screens in 3 modes\nPASS  All ten screenshots the plan lists exist and were reviewed\nPASS  pixel-engine.js byte-identical to the kit\nPASS  About and Credits filled in\nPASS  exit-interview.json parses, right shape, every answer present\nPASS  About shows all 15 Source Information fields, Usage: added on import\nPASS  Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback each start on their own page\nPASS  The brief's questions, in order, each answered and on screen\nPASS  Credits: Pixel Engine, three.js, Rapier, the AI Hub client, not affiliated\n