{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgmiua6Z9ox5qzuFKqvZ5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QvtUVxUTfkVbeM2FSFyYdh","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162030__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","offset":85,"limit":195,"allow_large":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2296,"cache_read_input_tokens":436813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2296},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98060110-4ead-4574-964f-daef0a492581","uuid":"be564353-a250-4380-b22c-469d8d3a1d67","timestamp":"2026-11-06T13:05:40.089Z","request_id":"req_011CgmiuZfGDbr47ogfMKr4L","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QvtUVxUTfkVbeM2FSFyYdh":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162030__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","offset":85,"limit":195,"allow_large":true}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QvtUVxUTfkVbeM2FSFyYdh","type":"tool_result","content":"85\t## 3. Verified engine facts\n86\t\n87\tThe kit's guide is written with a platformer in mind. These are the traps and answers for a first-person open-world game. I ran these unless a line says \"not run\". Read `GUIDE.md` whole as the kit asks, and `ASSETS.md` before writing models; search `API.md`, don't read it whole.\n88\t\n89\t**Single file on `file://` works.** The kit says modules don't load from `file://`, but that only applies to separate files. An inline `<script type=\"module\">` holding the esbuild bundle of game plus engine boots on `file://`, with `__PIXEL_READY__` resolving in 2 to 3 s (software WebGPU, 1280 m terrain, 150k grass blades). See section 23.\n90\t\n91\t**First-person traps:**\n92\t\n93\t| Trap | Fix |\n94\t| --- | --- |\n95\t| The `first` rig's far plane is 160 m | In `setup`: `const cam = ctx.camera.camera; cam.far = 1000; cam.updateProjectionMatrix();` |\n96\t| The rig turns the view while KeyE or KeyQ is held, which breaks PC use keys | Replace the instance's `step` (below). Re-install after any `engine.setCamera`, in `onCameraChange` |\n97\t| `input.wantsPointerLock` is overwritten every frame while the preset is `first`, so menu clicks grab the pointer | Define an accessor (below) |\n98\t| Default pad mapping presses keyboard codes (R2 = KeyC, Square = KeyJ...) | Replace `input.gamepadButtons` with `Pad*` codes (below). `isDown`, `wasPressed` and `consumePress` then work on them and keep the devices apart |\n99\t| Right stick arrives as `input.mouseDelta` at `gamepadLookSpeed` (500) px/s, 0.0025 rad per unit | Keep that path so recordings still work. Scale by device in your `step` |\n100\t| `engine.paused` didn't stop anything under `step()` | Pause with `engine.timeScale = 0`. `update` still runs with `dt = 0`, `wasPressed` and `isDown` work, `fixedUpdate` stops. So menus live in `update`; animate them with your own frame counter, not `dt` |\n101\t| Esc under pointer lock may never reach you as a keydown (browser behaviour, not run here) | Open the pause menu on `pointerlockchange` to null during play |\n102\t| `requestPointerLock()` can reject shortly after Esc, and an unhandled rejection fails the hub check (browser behaviour, not run here; the accessor above I ran, but not with a real click) | Always `.catch(() => {})`. Re-lock from a `pointerup` listener when the game wants the lock. Test a real click on a menu item early |\n103\t\n104\t```js\n105\t// src/core/look.js: call in setup() and in onCameraChange() when the preset is 'first'\n106\texport function installLook(ctx, cam) {          // cam: your own state { override, enabled, sensX, sensY, invertY }\n107\t  ctx.camera.step = function (u) {\n108\t    if (cam.override) {                          // scenes, attract, chase camera\n109\t      this.camera.position.set(...cam.override.position);\n110\t      this.camera.lookAt(...cam.override.target);\n111\t      this.focus.copy(this.camera.position);\n112\t      return;\n113\t    }\n114\t    const k = cam.enabled ? 1 : 0;               // 0 while a menu is open or the map cursor has the stick\n115\t    this.yaw -= u.input.mouseDelta.x * 0.0025 * cam.sensX * k;\n116\t    this.pitch = Math.max(-1.45, Math.min(1.45, this.pitch - u.input.mouseDelta.y * 0.0025 * cam.sensY * (cam.invertY ? -1 : 1) * k));\n117\t    this.focus.copy(u.target);\n118\t    this.camera.position.copy(u.eye);            // from your game's eyePosition(ctx)\n119\t    this.camera.rotation.set(this.pitch, this.yaw, 0);\n120\t  };\n121\t}\n122\t// In setup():\n123\tctx.input.gamepadButtons = { 0:'PadCross', 1:'PadCircle', 2:'PadSquare', 3:'PadTriangle', 4:'PadL1', 5:'PadR1', 6:'PadL2', 7:'PadR2',\n124\t  8:'PadCreate', 9:'PadOptions', 10:'PadL3', 11:'PadR3', 12:'PadUp', 13:'PadDown', 14:'PadLeft', 15:'PadRight' };\n125\tlet wantLock = false;\n126\tObject.defineProperty(ctx.input, 'wantsPointerLock', { get: () => wantLock, set() {}, configurable: true });\n127\t```\n128\t\n129\t**Things attached to the camera.** `scene.add(cam); cam.add(viewModelGroup);` follows the camera with no lag. Give the group `setLookLayer(group, 'actors')` and `castShadow = false`. The near plane is 0.05 m.\n130\t\n131\t**The map in your hands works.** A `THREE.DataTexture` (RGBA `Uint8Array`, nearest filter, `colorSpace = SRGBColorSpace`) on a `PlaneGeometry` with `toonMaterial(0xffffff, { map })`, parented to the camera, reads crisp. It needs no DOM, so it also runs in Node. Draw into the array yourself.\n132\t\n133\t**Terrain.**\n134\t- `new Terrain({ size:[1280,1280], cells:[512,512], bands, steep, rock })` generates in about 0.4 s. Rapier's heightfield attach takes about 15 ms.\n135\t- The mesh is non-indexed: 6 vertices per cell at index `6 * (ix + iz * nx)`, with a 3-component `Float32` `color` attribute.\n136\t- Recolour a cell with `col.setXYZ(base + k, r, g, b)`, using components of `new THREE.Color(hex)`.\n137\t- Then set `col.addUpdateRange(start, count)` (in array elements) and `col.needsUpdate = true`. If a later update doesn't show, drop the ranges and upload the whole attribute at up to 4 Hz. Never call `land.update()` after painting: it recomputes the colours.\n138\t- Add 40 to every height so the ground stays above y = 0 (fog and maths are simpler).\n139\t\n140\t**Sky.** `applySky(engine, skyAt(hour))` drives sun and ambient light well, but its sky colours are cartoon cyan by day. Keep its sun and ambient, and draw your own dome:\n141\t```js\n142\tconst dome = new Mesh(new SphereGeometry(900, 24, 16), new MeshBasicNodeMaterial({ vertexColors: true, side: BackSide, fog: false }));\n143\tdome.frustumCulled = false; dome.castShadow = dome.receiveShadow = false; scene.add(dome);   // per frame: dome.position.copy(camera position)\n144\t```\n145\tGood verified gradient for 16:30: top `#7fa6b8`, middle `#e9c98f`, horizon `#d98a4e`. Fill in the hour table from section 6.\n146\t\n147\t**Fog.** In `groundFog`, `top` is world Y and everything below it sits in thick mist (it looks like a lake). For distance haze only: `groundFog({ color, top: -60, amount: 0.55, near: 200, far: 950 })`, then `scene.fogNode = fog.node`.\n148\t\n149\t**Grass.** `GrassField` is an `InstancedMesh` and honours `setMatrixAt`. `geometry.getAttribute('aRoot')` holds `[x, z, phase, height]` per blade. To burn a blade: `setMatrixAt(i, zeroScale)`, then `instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true`. Verified good look: `height:[0.55,1.2], width:0.13, base:PALETTE.orange, tip:PALETTE.sand`, about 3 blades per m².\n150\t\n151\t**Flames.** The library's `fire-burst` is an explosion ring, not grass fire. Write your own strip. In `textureDef`, `size` is the whole strip, frames are equal slices, and each frame's `pixels` layer is placed with `at:[i*w,0]`:\n152\t```js\n153\ttextureDef({ id:'grass-flame', about:'Licking grass flame', tags:['fx'], size:[40,14], frames:4, palette:{a:'red',b:'orange',c:'sand',d:'white'},\n154\t  layers:[0,1,2,3].map(i => ({ at:[i*10,0], pixels:FRAMES[i], legend:{a:'a',b:'b',c:'c',d:'d'} })) });\n155\t// new SpriteBatch(spriteSheet(def), { capacity: 1800 }); batch.set(i, { frame }) every ~0.1 s\n156\t```\n157\tSprites of this sheet at 1.6 to 3.2 m look right. By night, one `ctx.lights.request({ color:0xff7a30, intensity:40, radius:60, flicker:'torch' })` over a fire front turns the grass deep red, which is very good.\n158\t\n159\t**People.**\n160\t- A `model()` with nested `pivot:true` groups named as `HERO_RIG`'s joints works with the hero's clips: `compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, { ...HERO_RIG, spine: undefined }, build.object)`.\n161\t- `new Ragdoll(physics, object, HERO_RAGDOLL)` also works on it. It costs about 15 draws per person.\n162\t- Use this soldier as your starting point (as written it passes `checkModel` with no problems or warnings; shoulders about 1.12 m, head top about 1.74 m, faces +Z):\n163\t```js\n164\tconst limb = (name, len, w, c, kids = [], at = [0,0,0]) => ({ group:[{ box:[w,len,w], at:[0,-len/2,0], c }, ...kids], at, pivot:true, name });\n165\tconst foot = (name) => ({ group:[{ box:[0.12,0.1,0.26], at:[0,-0.05,0.05], c:'boot' }], at:[0,-0.22,0], pivot:true, name });\n166\texport const soldier = model({ id:'soldier', about:'Soldier on the hero rig', tags:['actor'], floats:true, collider:'none',\n167\t  palette:{ cloth:'slate', dark:'night', skin:'plum', boot:'ink', beret:'navy', strap:'white' },\n168\t  parts:[{ group:[\n169\t    { box:[0.34,0.2,0.2], at:[0,0.06,0], c:'dark' },\n170\t    { group:[\n171\t      { box:[0.38,0.5,0.22], at:[0,0.25,0], c:'cloth' },\n172\t      { box:[0.06,0.56,0.24], at:[0.1,0.25,0], rot:[0,0,28], c:'strap' },\n173\t      { group:[{ box:[0.2,0.22,0.2], at:[0,0.11,0], c:'skin' }, { box:[0.24,0.07,0.24], at:[0.01,0.25,0], rot:[0,0,-8], c:'beret' }], at:[0,0.52,0], pivot:true, name:'Head' },\n174\t      limb('ArmR',0.25,0.11,'cloth',[limb('ForearmR',0.16,0.09,'cloth',[limb('HandR',0.08,0.08,'skin',[],[0,-0.16,0])],[0,-0.25,0])],[-0.25,0.47,0]),\n175\t      limb('ArmL',0.25,0.11,'cloth',[limb('ForearmL',0.16,0.09,'cloth',[limb('HandL',0.08,0.08,'skin',[],[0,-0.16,0])],[0,-0.25,0])],[0.25,0.47,0]),\n176\t    ], at:[0,0.04,0], pivot:true, name:'Torso' },\n177\t    limb('LegR',0.3,0.13,'dark',[limb('ShinR',0.22,0.11,'dark',[foot('FootR')],[0,-0.3,0])],[-0.14,0,0]),\n178\t    limb('LegL',0.3,0.13,'dark',[limb('ShinL',0.22,0.11,'dark',[foot('FootL')],[0,-0.3,0])],[0.14,0,0]),\n179\t  ], at:[0,0.62,0], pivot:true, name:'Pelvis' }] });\n180\t```\n181\t\n182\t**Custom rigs work too,** with `p` offsets on any joint. For first-person hands, use a rig like `{ joints:['Root','Weapon','Mag','Bolt','ArmR','HandR','ArmL','HandL'], root:'Root', mirror:{}, soles:[], trace:[], limits:{}, fps:30 }`, a model with those pivot names, `compileClips`, and a `THREE.AnimationMixer`. A key looks like `[6, { Weapon:{ r:[0,0,-35], p:[-.05,.06,.05] } }, 'out']`.\n183\t\n184\t**Checks.** Use plain distance tests in `fixedUpdate` for sites and pickups: the engine triggers' `tag:'character'` belongs to the platformer hero.\n185\t\n186\t**Movement.** `CharacterController` stays grounded on the heightfield and is right for a grounded soldier. `PlatformerCharacter` (triple jumps, ground pounds) is not. `speed` is set in the constructor and read as a plain field; `setInput(move, jump, jumpHeld)` takes a move vector of length up to 1.\n187\t\n188\t**Vehicles.** `new Vehicle(physics, { size:[0.95,0.45,2.2], mass:12, power:160, topSpeed:26, wheelRadius:0.42, suspension:0.45, stiffness:30, grip:2.6, at, yaw })` reaches 22 m/s in 3 s on the terrain and stays upright. The defaults only reach 7 to 11 m/s on the same ground.\n189\t\n190\t**Navigation.** `new NavGrid(640, 640, { cell:2, origin:[-640,0,-640] })`: a long `path()` took 26 ms, a short one 2 ms, and one `flowTo()` 114 ms. So use `path` with a budget (at most 2 per fixed step, queued) and never use flow fields here.\n191\t\n192\t**HUD.** 480×270 art pixels at 16:9. Font is 5×7 with a 6 px advance, drawn uppercase. It has exactly: A-Z, 0-9, space and ``. , : ; ! ? ' \" - + = / ( ) [ ] { } % _ # & * @ < > | \\ ~ ^ $ ` ° ·`` plus curly quotes. There is **no em dash or ellipsis**, so write `-` and `...`; unknown characters draw as `?`. Scale 1 is about 28 px tall on a 1080p screen, which I judged readable as the smallest size. `hud.width` follows the screen's aspect, so anchor everything. `hud.text` doesn't wrap: write your own wrapper.\n193\t\n194\t**Audio.**\n195\t- Default volumes: master 0.8, sfx 1, music 0.6, persisted by the engine. `resetVolumes()` returns to those, so implement your own reset.\n196\t- `renderSound(def)` is pure and works in Node.\n197\t- `audio.play(defOrName, { pitch, volume, pan })` and `audio.loop(...)` with `voice.set({ volume, pan, muffle, pitch })` cover everything.\n198\t- No audio files are needed, and none are allowed.\n199\t\n200\t**Tests in Node.**\n201\t- `Engine.headless(createGame(), { camera:{ preset:'first' }, viewport:{ width:1920, height:1080, dpr:1 } })` runs at far faster than real time (300 steps took about 60 ms on a bare scene).\n202\t- A pad works: `globalThis.navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`, with `pad = { connected:true, mapping:'standard', axes:[0,0,0,0], buttons:[...{pressed,value}] }`.\n203\t- Mouse works: `input.setPointer(x, y, down)`, read back through `input.buttonEvents`; `input.addPointer(dx, dy, wheel)` adds movement.\n204\t- `spriteSheet`, `textureMap`, `buildModel` and the HUD all work in Node. Only `drawSheet` needs a canvas: don't use it.\n205\t\n206\t**Hub.** I ran the real harness against an engine-weight shared-world page, with `card:true` and with the `LOOK` from section 6 for the two-player runs. All four scenarios the hub uses pass in 12 to 17 s, with `AIHub.ready()` called about 3 s after load (one straggler took 6.6 s):\n207\t- `players=2&auto=1`\n208\t- `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\n209\t- the same four-player settings with `&scenario=host-leave`\n210\t- `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\n211\t\n212\tA software-rendered copy only draws about 2 frames a second there, so **the net tick and the card's Enter key must not depend on the render loop** (section 19). For the server copy, `await Engine.headless(game, {})` in the browser page plus `setInterval(() => e.step(6, 1/60), 100)` works and needs no GPU.\n213\t\n214\t**Same-computer link.** `BroadcastChannel` works between two `file://` pages of one browser profile (verified).\n215\t\n216\t**A local server, when you need one.** `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` buffers its \"port\" line when its stdout is a pipe, so a script waiting for it hangs forever. Run it as `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, or print the port some other way.\n217\t\n218\t**Software rendering runs at about 1 fps here.** That is expected. Treat the performance budget as unlimited: add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows as a scene needs. FPS targets and optimization are out of scope and handled later. Never judge the game by real time in a headless browser. Drive tests with `e.step(n)` and read state. For a real picture, set `e.manual = false`, wait 10 s or more, and take a Playwright `page.screenshot`. That produced correct frames for me with the flags in section 24.\n219\t\n220\t---\n221\t\n222\t## 4. Architecture\n223\t\n224\tUnzip the kit **into the dev folder root** so `pixel-engine.js` and `check.mjs` sit at its top. The kit's tools find the engine by looking upward, so `node check.mjs --headless src/game.js` and `node check.mjs --sheets src/art/models.js` then just work. Never edit `pixel-engine.js`. Replace the kit's `game.js` and `index.html` with yours, and delete its `examples/` and `packs/` when you are done with them: the game loads no packs, so the credits and the bundle stay simple.\n225\t\n226\t```\n227\tpixel-engine.js check.mjs GUIDE.md API.md ...   the kit, as shipped\n228\tindex.html                the dev page: same <head> order as the bundle (section 23), loads src/main.js\n229\tvendor/session.js         AI Hub client, unmodified (downloaded from the hub's repository)\n230\tvendor/hub/               harness.html bridge.js round.js (tests only, never bundled)\n231\ttools/build.mjs           esbuild + inline: writes the one HTML file your brief names\n232\tsrc/main.js               browser boot only: hub connect, touch check, Engine.start\n233\tsrc/game.js               export createGame(options); the Game hooks; the mode machine; no DOM at import\n234\tsrc/core/                 actions.js look.js tuning.js save.js rng.js state.js events.js\n235\tsrc/world/                terrain.js roads.js sites.js grass.js sky.js fire.js wind.js map-texture.js\n236\tsrc/actors/               player.js soldier.js squad.js buddy.js civilian.js fauna.js rig.js\n237\tsrc/combat/               weapons.js projectiles.js damage.js viewmodel.js\n238\tsrc/body/                 health.js fever.js surgery.js\n239\tsrc/vehicles/             vehicle.js driving.js repair.js train.js\n240\tsrc/missions/             runner.js steps.js scripts.js ledger.js\n241\tsrc/ui/                   hud.js menu.js screens/*.js glyphs.js prompts.js dialogue.js pixfont.js touchgate.js about.js\n242\tsrc/dev/                  devmode.js sliders.js overlays.js\n243\tsrc/attract/              director.js beats.js\n244\tsrc/net/                  session.js hubSession.js linkSession.js soloSession.js round.js host.js client.js card.js\n245\tsrc/audio/                sfx.js music.js voice.js ambience.js\n246\tsrc/art/                  models.js textures.js viewmodels.js clips.js   (data only: exports for check.mjs --sheets)\n247\tsrc/content/              title.js weapons.js sites.js roads.js missions.js dialogue.js notes.js about.js tuning-defaults.js\n248\ttests/                    run.mjs lib/ suites/\n249\t```\n250\t\n251\t**Patterns to follow:**\n252\t- **One Game object, one Engine.** `createGame()` builds the world once. Title, attract, campaign and round are modes on it (`game.mode`). Don't use `engine.loadGame` between them: it would throw away the terrain. For New Game and Attract use `world.reset()`, which clears fire, sites and actors and repaints the base colours.\n253\t- **Data over code.** All content lives in `src/content/` and `src/art/` as plain data. Adding a weapon, site, mission, line of dialogue or model must not need a code change. This is what lets the rest of the game be built later \"with normal designer tools\": tables, the sheets tool and dev mode's placement readout. Keep a one-page `DESIGNERS.md` in the dev folder saying how to add each.\n254\t- **Actions, not keys.** `src/core/actions.js` maps an action to its pad code and its keyboard/mouse codes (section 16). All gameplay asks for actions. In `fixedUpdate` use `input.consumeAny(codes)`; in `update` use `wasPressed`.\n255\t- **Gameplay in `fixedUpdate`, presentation in `update`.** All randomness comes from `ctx.random.fork(name)`, never `Math.random`. Cosmetic forks are made in `setup`.\n256\t- **`TUNING`.** Every balance number lives in `src/content/tuning-defaults.js`, copied to a live `TUNING` object. Systems read `TUNING.x` at the moment of use and never cache it, so sliders act instantly.\n257\t- **`game.state()`.** One plain JSON snapshot for every test: `{ mode, screen, focus, device, glyphSet, notice, attract, mission, step, hp, stones, tablets, syrettes, weapon:{id,cond,jammed,mag}, fever, pos, yaw, vehicle, hour, wind, fire:{burning,burnt}, prompts:[...], subtitle, buddies, ledger }`. Expose it in `ready(ctx)` as `window.__GAME__ = game` when `window` exists. Give `status()` a useful one-liner and `fingerprint()` a hash of hp, stones, weapon state, fire counts and actor positions.\n258\t- **Sim and view apart.** A soldier is data plus an optional view; a fire cell is data plus sprites. In a round, a client's soldiers are puppets with no brain and no collider, driven by snapshots. Write them this way from the first day, because retrofitting it is the expensive part of section 19.\n259\t- **Simulation bubble.** Sites within 260 m of the player are live; others are counters. Vehicles on patrol are simulated within 400 m. This keeps the code simple and headless tests fast.\n260\t- **Guard the page.** Any code that touches `window`, `document`, `navigator` or `localStorage` checks that it exists. `src/game.js` must import cleanly in Node.\n261\t- **Issues log.** Keep `tests/ISSUES.md`: one line per bug testing catches, marked critical or not. Your brief asks how many there were at the end.\n262\t\n263\t**Working rules from your brief:** keep every file inside your own folder (not `/tmp`, not a parent). Don't read other agents' folders. Other agents share this machine, so keep to one or two browsers at once and close them. Don't run `playwright install`; use `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder. `uv run --with pillow ...` is the way to get Python packages. Stop only processes you started, by PID, and never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`. \"Target closed\" or \"page crashed\" means memory ran out: run fewer browsers rather than retrying. If you serve anything, confirm the page's title is your game before trusting a result.\n264\t\n265\t---\n266\t\n267\t## 5. World\n268\t\n269\tThe whole game happens on one continuous 1280 × 1280 m terrain (x and z from -640 to 640, +x east, -z north, 512 × 512 cells at 2.5 m). Heights have a +40 offset.\n270\t\n271\t**Height function,** built up in this order:\n272\t1. `40 + 14*fbm(x*.0032, z*.0032) + 2.5*fbm(x*.02, z*.02)`.\n273\t2. The north ridge: up to +42 m with ridged noise around (-130, -520), radius 260.\n274\t3. The west escarpment: rising +30 m for x < -520, cut by the Pass at (-590, -430).\n275\t4. The salt pan: flattened to y = 36 around (200, 470), radius 150.\n276\t5. The mine: a terraced pit at (150, -300), radius 70, 24 m deep.\n277\t6. The river: a channel along its polyline, 14 m wide and 3 m below the banks.\n278\t7. The stamps: flatten around each site (radial falloff) and along each road (5 m wide, 3 m shoulder).\n279\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98060110-4ead-4574-964f-daef0a492581","uuid":"a02cd30a-ce72-4008-9745-f5e7e01c5aa4","timestamp":"2026-11-06T13:05:40.099Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162030__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","content":"## 3. Verified engine facts\n\nThe kit's guide is written with a platformer in mind. These are the traps and answers for a first-person open-world game. I ran these unless a line says \"not run\". Read `GUIDE.md` whole as the kit asks, and `ASSETS.md` before writing models; search `API.md`, don't read it whole.\n\n**Single file on `file://` works.** The kit says modules don't load from `file://`, but that only applies to separate files. An inline `<script type=\"module\">` holding the esbuild bundle of game plus engine boots on `file://`, with `__PIXEL_READY__` resolving in 2 to 3 s (software WebGPU, 1280 m terrain, 150k grass blades). See section 23.\n\n**First-person traps:**\n\n| Trap | Fix |\n| --- | --- |\n| The `first` rig's far plane is 160 m | In `setup`: `const cam = ctx.camera.camera; cam.far = 1000; cam.updateProjectionMatrix();` |\n| The rig turns the view while KeyE or KeyQ is held, which breaks PC use keys | Replace the instance's `step` (below). Re-install after any `engine.setCamera`, in `onCameraChange` |\n| `input.wantsPointerLock` is overwritten every frame while the preset is `first`, so menu clicks grab the pointer | Define an accessor (below) |\n| Default pad mapping presses keyboard codes (R2 = KeyC, Square = KeyJ...) | Replace `input.gamepadButtons` with `Pad*` codes (below). `isDown`, `wasPressed` and `consumePress` then work on them and keep the devices apart |\n| Right stick arrives as `input.mouseDelta` at `gamepadLookSpeed` (500) px/s, 0.0025 rad per unit | Keep that path so recordings still work. Scale by device in your `step` |\n| `engine.paused` didn't stop anything under `step()` | Pause with `engine.timeScale = 0`. `update` still runs with `dt = 0`, `wasPressed` and `isDown` work, `fixedUpdate` stops. So menus live in `update`; animate them with your own frame counter, not `dt` |\n| Esc under pointer lock may never reach you as a keydown (browser behaviour, not run here) | Open the pause menu on `pointerlockchange` to null during play |\n| `requestPointerLock()` can reject shortly after Esc, and an unhandled rejection fails the hub check (browser behaviour, not run here; the accessor above I ran, but not with a real click) | Always `.catch(() => {})`. Re-lock from a `pointerup` listener when the game wants the lock. Test a real click on a menu item early |\n\n```js\n// src/core/look.js: call in setup() and in onCameraChange() when the preset is 'first'\nexport function installLook(ctx, cam) {          // cam: your own state { override, enabled, sensX, sensY, invertY }\n  ctx.camera.step = function (u) {\n    if (cam.override) {                          // scenes, attract, chase camera\n      this.camera.position.set(...cam.override.position);\n      this.camera.lookAt(...cam.override.target);\n      this.focus.copy(this.camera.position);\n      return;\n    }\n    const k = cam.enabled ? 1 : 0;               // 0 while a menu is open or the map cursor has the stick\n    this.yaw -= u.input.mouseDelta.x * 0.0025 * cam.sensX * k;\n    this.pitch = Math.max(-1.45, Math.min(1.45, this.pitch - u.input.mouseDelta.y * 0.0025 * cam.sensY * (cam.invertY ? -1 : 1) * k));\n    this.focus.copy(u.target);\n    this.camera.position.copy(u.eye);            // from your game's eyePosition(ctx)\n    this.camera.rotation.set(this.pitch, this.yaw, 0);\n  };\n}\n// In setup():\nctx.input.gamepadButtons = { 0:'PadCross', 1:'PadCircle', 2:'PadSquare', 3:'PadTriangle', 4:'PadL1', 5:'PadR1', 6:'PadL2', 7:'PadR2',\n  8:'PadCreate', 9:'PadOptions', 10:'PadL3', 11:'PadR3', 12:'PadUp', 13:'PadDown', 14:'PadLeft', 15:'PadRight' };\nlet wantLock = false;\nObject.defineProperty(ctx.input, 'wantsPointerLock', { get: () => wantLock, set() {}, configurable: true });\n```\n\n**Things attached to the camera.** `scene.add(cam); cam.add(viewModelGroup);` follows the camera with no lag. Give the group `setLookLayer(group, 'actors')` and `castShadow = false`. The near plane is 0.05 m.\n\n**The map in your hands works.** A `THREE.DataTexture` (RGBA `Uint8Array`, nearest filter, `colorSpace = SRGBColorSpace`) on a `PlaneGeometry` with `toonMaterial(0xffffff, { map })`, parented to the camera, reads crisp. It needs no DOM, so it also runs in Node. Draw into the array yourself.\n\n**Terrain.**\n- `new Terrain({ size:[1280,1280], cells:[512,512], bands, steep, rock })` generates in about 0.4 s. Rapier's heightfield attach takes about 15 ms.\n- The mesh is non-indexed: 6 vertices per cell at index `6 * (ix + iz * nx)`, with a 3-component `Float32` `color` attribute.\n- Recolour a cell with `col.setXYZ(base + k, r, g, b)`, using components of `new THREE.Color(hex)`.\n- Then set `col.addUpdateRange(start, count)` (in array elements) and `col.needsUpdate = true`. If a later update doesn't show, drop the ranges and upload the whole attribute at up to 4 Hz. Never call `land.update()` after painting: it recomputes the colours.\n- Add 40 to every height so the ground stays above y = 0 (fog and maths are simpler).\n\n**Sky.** `applySky(engine, skyAt(hour))` drives sun and ambient light well, but its sky colours are cartoon cyan by day. Keep its sun and ambient, and draw your own dome:\n```js\nconst dome = new Mesh(new SphereGeometry(900, 24, 16), new MeshBasicNodeMaterial({ vertexColors: true, side: BackSide, fog: false }));\ndome.frustumCulled = false; dome.castShadow = dome.receiveShadow = false; scene.add(dome);   // per frame: dome.position.copy(camera position)\n```\nGood verified gradient for 16:30: top `#7fa6b8`, middle `#e9c98f`, horizon `#d98a4e`. Fill in the hour table from section 6.\n\n**Fog.** In `groundFog`, `top` is world Y and everything below it sits in thick mist (it looks like a lake). For distance haze only: `groundFog({ color, top: -60, amount: 0.55, near: 200, far: 950 })`, then `scene.fogNode = fog.node`.\n\n**Grass.** `GrassField` is an `InstancedMesh` and honours `setMatrixAt`. `geometry.getAttribute('aRoot')` holds `[x, z, phase, height]` per blade. To burn a blade: `setMatrixAt(i, zeroScale)`, then `instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true`. Verified good look: `height:[0.55,1.2], width:0.13, base:PALETTE.orange, tip:PALETTE.sand`, about 3 blades per m².\n\n**Flames.** The library's `fire-burst` is an explosion ring, not grass fire. Write your own strip. In `textureDef`, `size` is the whole strip, frames are equal slices, and each frame's `pixels` layer is placed with `at:[i*w,0]`:\n```js\ntextureDef({ id:'grass-flame', about:'Licking grass flame', tags:['fx'], size:[40,14], frames:4, palette:{a:'red',b:'orange',c:'sand',d:'white'},\n  layers:[0,1,2,3].map(i => ({ at:[i*10,0], pixels:FRAMES[i], legend:{a:'a',b:'b',c:'c',d:'d'} })) });\n// new SpriteBatch(spriteSheet(def), { capacity: 1800 }); batch.set(i, { frame }) every ~0.1 s\n```\nSprites of this sheet at 1.6 to 3.2 m look right. By night, one `ctx.lights.request({ color:0xff7a30, intensity:40, radius:60, flicker:'torch' })` over a fire front turns the grass deep red, which is very good.\n\n**People.**\n- A `model()` with nested `pivot:true` groups named as `HERO_RIG`'s joints works with the hero's clips: `compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, { ...HERO_RIG, spine: undefined }, build.object)`.\n- `new Ragdoll(physics, object, HERO_RAGDOLL)` also works on it. It costs about 15 draws per person.\n- Use this soldier as your starting point (as written it passes `checkModel` with no problems or warnings; shoulders about 1.12 m, head top about 1.74 m, faces +Z):\n```js\nconst limb = (name, len, w, c, kids = [], at = [0,0,0]) => ({ group:[{ box:[w,len,w], at:[0,-len/2,0], c }, ...kids], at, pivot:true, name });\nconst foot = (name) => ({ group:[{ box:[0.12,0.1,0.26], at:[0,-0.05,0.05], c:'boot' }], at:[0,-0.22,0], pivot:true, name });\nexport const soldier = model({ id:'soldier', about:'Soldier on the hero rig', tags:['actor'], floats:true, collider:'none',\n  palette:{ cloth:'slate', dark:'night', skin:'plum', boot:'ink', beret:'navy', strap:'white' },\n  parts:[{ group:[\n    { box:[0.34,0.2,0.2], at:[0,0.06,0], c:'dark' },\n    { group:[\n      { box:[0.38,0.5,0.22], at:[0,0.25,0], c:'cloth' },\n      { box:[0.06,0.56,0.24], at:[0.1,0.25,0], rot:[0,0,28], c:'strap' },\n      { group:[{ box:[0.2,0.22,0.2], at:[0,0.11,0], c:'skin' }, { box:[0.24,0.07,0.24], at:[0.01,0.25,0], rot:[0,0,-8], c:'beret' }], at:[0,0.52,0], pivot:true, name:'Head' },\n      limb('ArmR',0.25,0.11,'cloth',[limb('ForearmR',0.16,0.09,'cloth',[limb('HandR',0.08,0.08,'skin',[],[0,-0.16,0])],[0,-0.25,0])],[-0.25,0.47,0]),\n      limb('ArmL',0.25,0.11,'cloth',[limb('ForearmL',0.16,0.09,'cloth',[limb('HandL',0.08,0.08,'skin',[],[0,-0.16,0])],[0,-0.25,0])],[0.25,0.47,0]),\n    ], at:[0,0.04,0], pivot:true, name:'Torso' },\n    limb('LegR',0.3,0.13,'dark',[limb('ShinR',0.22,0.11,'dark',[foot('FootR')],[0,-0.3,0])],[-0.14,0,0]),\n    limb('LegL',0.3,0.13,'dark',[limb('ShinL',0.22,0.11,'dark',[foot('FootL')],[0,-0.3,0])],[0.14,0,0]),\n  ], at:[0,0.62,0], pivot:true, name:'Pelvis' }] });\n```\n\n**Custom rigs work too,** with `p` offsets on any joint. For first-person hands, use a rig like `{ joints:['Root','Weapon','Mag','Bolt','ArmR','HandR','ArmL','HandL'], root:'Root', mirror:{}, soles:[], trace:[], limits:{}, fps:30 }`, a model with those pivot names, `compileClips`, and a `THREE.AnimationMixer`. A key looks like `[6, { Weapon:{ r:[0,0,-35], p:[-.05,.06,.05] } }, 'out']`.\n\n**Checks.** Use plain distance tests in `fixedUpdate` for sites and pickups: the engine triggers' `tag:'character'` belongs to the platformer hero.\n\n**Movement.** `CharacterController` stays grounded on the heightfield and is right for a grounded soldier. `PlatformerCharacter` (triple jumps, ground pounds) is not. `speed` is set in the constructor and read as a plain field; `setInput(move, jump, jumpHeld)` takes a move vector of length up to 1.\n\n**Vehicles.** `new Vehicle(physics, { size:[0.95,0.45,2.2], mass:12, power:160, topSpeed:26, wheelRadius:0.42, suspension:0.45, stiffness:30, grip:2.6, at, yaw })` reaches 22 m/s in 3 s on the terrain and stays upright. The defaults only reach 7 to 11 m/s on the same ground.\n\n**Navigation.** `new NavGrid(640, 640, { cell:2, origin:[-640,0,-640] })`: a long `path()` took 26 ms, a short one 2 ms, and one `flowTo()` 114 ms. So use `path` with a budget (at most 2 per fixed step, queued) and never use flow fields here.\n\n**HUD.** 480×270 art pixels at 16:9. Font is 5×7 with a 6 px advance, drawn uppercase. It has exactly: A-Z, 0-9, space and ``. , : ; ! ? ' \" - + = / ( ) [ ] { } % _ # & * @ < > | \\ ~ ^ $ ` ° ·`` plus curly quotes. There is **no em dash or ellipsis**, so write `-` and `...`; unknown characters draw as `?`. Scale 1 is about 28 px tall on a 1080p screen, which I judged readable as the smallest size. `hud.width` follows the screen's aspect, so anchor everything. `hud.text` doesn't wrap: write your own wrapper.\n\n**Audio.**\n- Default volumes: master 0.8, sfx 1, music 0.6, persisted by the engine. `resetVolumes()` returns to those, so implement your own reset.\n- `renderSound(def)` is pure and works in Node.\n- `audio.play(defOrName, { pitch, volume, pan })` and `audio.loop(...)` with `voice.set({ volume, pan, muffle, pitch })` cover everything.\n- No audio files are needed, and none are allowed.\n\n**Tests in Node.**\n- `Engine.headless(createGame(), { camera:{ preset:'first' }, viewport:{ width:1920, height:1080, dpr:1 } })` runs at far faster than real time (300 steps took about 60 ms on a bare scene).\n- A pad works: `globalThis.navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`, with `pad = { connected:true, mapping:'standard', axes:[0,0,0,0], buttons:[...{pressed,value}] }`.\n- Mouse works: `input.setPointer(x, y, down)`, read back through `input.buttonEvents`; `input.addPointer(dx, dy, wheel)` adds movement.\n- `spriteSheet`, `textureMap`, `buildModel` and the HUD all work in Node. Only `drawSheet` needs a canvas: don't use it.\n\n**Hub.** I ran the real harness against an engine-weight shared-world page, with `card:true` and with the `LOOK` from section 6 for the two-player runs. All four scenarios the hub uses pass in 12 to 17 s, with `AIHub.ready()` called about 3 s after load (one straggler took 6.6 s):\n- `players=2&auto=1`\n- `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\n- the same four-player settings with `&scenario=host-leave`\n- `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\n\nA software-rendered copy only draws about 2 frames a second there, so **the net tick and the card's Enter key must not depend on the render loop** (section 19). For the server copy, `await Engine.headless(game, {})` in the browser page plus `setInterval(() => e.step(6, 1/60), 100)` works and needs no GPU.\n\n**Same-computer link.** `BroadcastChannel` works between two `file://` pages of one browser profile (verified).\n\n**A local server, when you need one.** `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` buffers its \"port\" line when its stdout is a pipe, so a script waiting for it hangs forever. Run it as `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, or print the port some other way.\n\n**Software rendering runs at about 1 fps here.** That is expected. Treat the performance budget as unlimited: add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows as a scene needs. FPS targets and optimization are out of scope and handled later. Never judge the game by real time in a headless browser. Drive tests with `e.step(n)` and read state. For a real picture, set `e.manual = false`, wait 10 s or more, and take a Playwright `page.screenshot`. That produced correct frames for me with the flags in section 24.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\nUnzip the kit **into the dev folder root** so `pixel-engine.js` and `check.mjs` sit at its top. The kit's tools find the engine by looking upward, so `node check.mjs --headless src/game.js` and `node check.mjs --sheets src/art/models.js` then just work. Never edit `pixel-engine.js`. Replace the kit's `game.js` and `index.html` with yours, and delete its `examples/` and `packs/` when you are done with them: the game loads no packs, so the credits and the bundle stay simple.\n\n```\npixel-engine.js check.mjs GUIDE.md API.md ...   the kit, as shipped\nindex.html                the dev page: same <head> order as the bundle (section 23), loads src/main.js\nvendor/session.js         AI Hub client, unmodified (downloaded from the hub's repository)\nvendor/hub/               harness.html bridge.js round.js (tests only, never bundled)\ntools/build.mjs           esbuild + inline: writes the one HTML file your brief names\nsrc/main.js               browser boot only: hub connect, touch check, Engine.start\nsrc/game.js               export createGame(options); the Game hooks; the mode machine; no DOM at import\nsrc/core/                 actions.js look.js tuning.js save.js rng.js state.js events.js\nsrc/world/                terrain.js roads.js sites.js grass.js sky.js fire.js wind.js map-texture.js\nsrc/actors/               player.js soldier.js squad.js buddy.js civilian.js fauna.js rig.js\nsrc/combat/               weapons.js projectiles.js damage.js viewmodel.js\nsrc/body/                 health.js fever.js surgery.js\nsrc/vehicles/             vehicle.js driving.js repair.js train.js\nsrc/missions/             runner.js steps.js scripts.js ledger.js\nsrc/ui/                   hud.js menu.js screens/*.js glyphs.js prompts.js dialogue.js pixfont.js touchgate.js about.js\nsrc/dev/                  devmode.js sliders.js overlays.js\nsrc/attract/              director.js beats.js\nsrc/net/                  session.js hubSession.js linkSession.js soloSession.js round.js host.js client.js card.js\nsrc/audio/                sfx.js music.js voice.js ambience.js\nsrc/art/                  models.js textures.js viewmodels.js clips.js   (data only: exports for check.mjs --sheets)\nsrc/content/              title.js weapons.js sites.js roads.js missions.js dialogue.js notes.js about.js tuning-defaults.js\ntests/                    run.mjs lib/ suites/\n```\n\n**Patterns to follow:**\n- **One Game object, one Engine.** `createGame()` builds the world once. Title, attract, campaign and round are modes on it (`game.mode`). Don't use `engine.loadGame` between them: it would throw away the terrain. For New Game and Attract use `world.reset()`, which clears fire, sites and actors and repaints the base colours.\n- **Data over code.** All content lives in `src/content/` and `src/art/` as plain data. Adding a weapon, site, mission, line of dialogue or model must not need a code change. This is what lets the rest of the game be built later \"with normal designer tools\": tables, the sheets tool and dev mode's placement readout. Keep a one-page `DESIGNERS.md` in the dev folder saying how to add each.\n- **Actions, not keys.** `src/core/actions.js` maps an action to its pad code and its keyboard/mouse codes (section 16). All gameplay asks for actions. In `fixedUpdate` use `input.consumeAny(codes)`; in `update` use `wasPressed`.\n- **Gameplay in `fixedUpdate`, presentation in `update`.** All randomness comes from `ctx.random.fork(name)`, never `Math.random`. Cosmetic forks are made in `setup`.\n- **`TUNING`.** Every balance number lives in `src/content/tuning-defaults.js`, copied to a live `TUNING` object. Systems read `TUNING.x` at the moment of use and never cache it, so sliders act instantly.\n- **`game.state()`.** One plain JSON snapshot for every test: `{ mode, screen, focus, device, glyphSet, notice, attract, mission, step, hp, stones, tablets, syrettes, weapon:{id,cond,jammed,mag}, fever, pos, yaw, vehicle, hour, wind, fire:{burning,burnt}, prompts:[...], subtitle, buddies, ledger }`. Expose it in `ready(ctx)` as `window.__GAME__ = game` when `window` exists. Give `status()` a useful one-liner and `fingerprint()` a hash of hp, stones, weapon state, fire counts and actor positions.\n- **Sim and view apart.** A soldier is data plus an optional view; a fire cell is data plus sprites. In a round, a client's soldiers are puppets with no brain and no collider, driven by snapshots. Write them this way from the first day, because retrofitting it is the expensive part of section 19.\n- **Simulation bubble.** Sites within 260 m of the player are live; others are counters. Vehicles on patrol are simulated within 400 m. This keeps the code simple and headless tests fast.\n- **Guard the page.** Any code that touches `window`, `document`, `navigator` or `localStorage` checks that it exists. `src/game.js` must import cleanly in Node.\n- **Issues log.** Keep `tests/ISSUES.md`: one line per bug testing catches, marked critical or not. Your brief asks how many there were at the end.\n\n**Working rules from your brief:** keep every file inside your own folder (not `/tmp`, not a parent). Don't read other agents' folders. Other agents share this machine, so keep to one or two browsers at once and close them. Don't run `playwright install`; use `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder. `uv run --with pillow ...` is the way to get Python packages. Stop only processes you started, by PID, and never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`. \"Target closed\" or \"page crashed\" means memory ran out: run fewer browsers rather than retrying. If you serve anything, confirm the page's title is your game before trusting a result.\n\n---\n\n## 5. World\n\nThe whole game happens on one continuous 1280 × 1280 m terrain (x and z from -640 to 640, +x east, -z north, 512 × 512 cells at 2.5 m). Heights have a +40 offset.\n\n**Height function,** built up in this order:\n1. `40 + 14*fbm(x*.0032, z*.0032) + 2.5*fbm(x*.02, z*.02)`.\n2. The north ridge: up to +42 m with ridged noise around (-130, -520), radius 260.\n3. The west escarpment: rising +30 m for x < -520, cut by the Pass at (-590, -430).\n4. The salt pan: flattened to y = 36 around (200, 470), radius 150.\n5. The mine: a terraced pit at (150, -300), radius 70, 24 m deep.\n6. The river: a channel along its polyline, 14 m wide and 3 m below the banks.\n7. The stamps: flatten around each site (radial falloff) and along each road (5 m wide, 3 m shoulder).\n","numLines":195,"startLine":85,"totalLines":1129}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QvtUVxUTfkVbeM2FSFyYdh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgmiua6Z9ox5qzuFKqvZ5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FrZUWCGeZHiKUwgSMx7ofS","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162030__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","offset":1037,"limit":92,"allow_large":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2296,"cache_read_input_tokens":436813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2296},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98060110-4ead-4574-964f-daef0a492581","uuid":"9860a347-7b1a-44b0-9a75-90f849fbe61f","timestamp":"2026-11-06T13:05:41.887Z","request_id":"req_011CgmiuZfGDbr47ogfMKr4L","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01FrZUWCGeZHiKUwgSMx7ofS":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162030__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","offset":1037,"limit":92,"allow_large":true}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FrZUWCGeZHiKUwgSMx7ofS","type":"tool_result","content":"1037\t\n1038\t---\n1039\t\n1040\t## 25. Build order\n1041\t\n1042\tEach step ends with a playable build and passing tests, and leaves nothing half-done. Do them in order. Write sound, feedback and tests with each feature rather than at the end.\n1043\t\n1044\t| Step | Do | Done when |\n1045\t| --- | --- | --- |\n1046\t| 0 Pipeline | Pick the title. Unzip the kit. Download `vendor/`. `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs` with the hub suite. A blank `createGame()` with a shared-world round that does nothing | Single file opens on `file://`; **all four hub scenarios pass from day one**. Keep them passing |\n1047\t| 1 Walk | Terrain, roads, river, sky, wind, grass, trees. Player, `installLook`, actions, pad codes, device tracking, HUD basics, pause | You can walk the Kettle by pad or mouse |\n1048\t| 2 Shoot | Hands rig and `tractor` first, soldier rig, one AI squad, damage, health, syrette, wear, jam, reload. SFX for each | A fight at P2 is fun on its own. Add the other guns |\n1049\t| 3 Burn | Fire grid, flare, bottle, flamethrower, scars, regrowth, soldier panic, day and night. Fire sounds | Lighting a field at night makes you stop and watch |\n1050\t| 4 Drive | Vehicles, driving view, damage, repair, patrols, posts, train | Run a checkpoint in a pickup and break down after |\n1051\t| 5 World | All sites, safehouses, Tibor, stones, tracker, fever, tablets, surgery, monocular, held map, rail halts, trig points, save and load | A whole session without missions works |\n1052\t| 6 Story | Mission runner, dialogue, voices, buddies, rescues, M0 to End, Ledger, endings. Playthrough suite | A bot and you both finish it |\n1053\t| 7 Shell | Title, all menus, settings, glyph sets, About, touch gate, attract, dev mode | Every screen works by pad, keyboard and mouse |\n1054\t| 8 Multiplayer | Sim/view puppets, host, client, sessions, Cash Run, card, solo bots, link | Hub and link suites pass with a real round |\n1055\t| 9 Polish | All music, ambience, feel, art passes, balance with the sliders, screenshots | You'd show it to someone |\n1056\t| 10 Ship | Final build, About pages, exit interview, chat reply, `node tests/run.mjs --all` | Section 27 is all true |\n1057\t\n1058\t---\n1059\t\n1060\t## 26. Cut order\n1061\t\n1062\tIf you can't finish everything, cut whole things from the top of this list. Don't leave stubs, and remove all traces (menu entries, map marks, lines). Keep every feature in the demo reachable and shown.\n1063\t\n1064\t1. Fauna (vultures first).\n1065\t2. Same-computer link (keep hub and solo).\n1066\t3. The game's own hub card (use the hub's card: `card:false`).\n1067\t4. Chase camera; dust weather.\n1068\t5. Mercy Runs after the first; trig points down to 3; cases down to 6.\n1069\t6. M5 (Mags offers her alternate plan in M6 instead: get the field workers out first, for less pay).\n1070\t7. M4 (give the tracker in M3; use the MG in M7; Dov joins at the bar).\n1071\t8. M8 (the column just starts walking after M7).\n1072\t9. Shared-world → ghost → parallel (section 19).\n1073\t\n1074\tNever cut: fire, wear and jams, fever, the held map, buddy rescue and the finish-it choice, Harrow's twist, dev mode with sliders, attract mode, glyphs and native mouse, touch gate, the hub check, music at 10%, the voices.\n1075\t\n1076\t---\n1077\t\n1078\t## 27. Done, reports, coverage\n1079\t\n1080\t**Done means:** every line below is true and you've checked it.\n1081\t- [ ] Your title, chosen by the brief's procedure, appears on the title screen and in `<title>`.\n1082\t- [ ] The one HTML file runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it.\n1083\t- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs --all` reports 0 failed.\n1084\t- [ ] All four hub scenarios pass, and the game still runs outside a session.\n1085\t- [ ] You finished the game yourself by bot, both endings.\n1086\t- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features anywhere.\n1087\t- [ ] Every screen shows native prompts for pad (PlayStation and Xbox glyphs) and keyboard/mouse.\n1088\t- [ ] You looked at the screenshots.\n1089\t- [ ] `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the copy in the kit's zip (keep the zip in `vendor/` and compare in the `static` suite).\n1090\t\n1091\t**About and Credits screen** (in-game, paged, reached from main menu and pause; text in `src/content/about.js`). Your brief marks this as critical. It must show:\n1092\t- **Source Information,** with every field your brief lists: Date (from your brief), Source (Far Cry 2), Plan (the file name of this plan as you received it), Genre and Perspective, Tech (engine and its version, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, esbuild, your additions), Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model (from your brief), and Usage: *added on import*. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n1093\t- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback:** your brief's questions and your honest answers, in order, each on its own page. Don't estimate cost.\n1094\t- **Credits:** Pixel Engine (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), the AI Hub session client, and \"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\".\n1095\t\n1096\t**Your final chat reply** repeats the same Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback.\n1097\t\n1098\t**The exit interview file** your brief specifies goes beside the HTML, in the exact JSON shape it gives, every value filled, and must parse. Suggested catalogue values: type `Game`; genre `Shooter`; source `Far Cry 2`; series `Far Cry`; players `1–4`; tags such as `first-person`, `pixel art`, `co-op`, `versus`, `vehicles`, `story`; the name is your title.\n1099\t\n1100\t**Where each line of your brief is covered:**\n1101\t\n1102\t| Brief item | Section |\n1103\t| --- | --- |\n1104\t| Source, original game, capture world, characters, themes, tone | 1, 2, 15 |\n1105\t| Genre, perspective | 1 |\n1106\t| Title by random pick (left to you) | top, 25 |\n1107\t| Research | 3 (done for you; research more if you want) |\n1108\t| Single HTML package, CDN allowance, bundle into root | 23 |\n1109\t| Engine kit kept as shipped, systems added on top | 3, 4, 23 |\n1110\t| Art direction: native base, new assets, library for generic props | 20 |\n1111\t| Animation: native base, new animations | 21 |\n1112\t| Audio in code, Animalese voices, music 10% | 22 |\n1113\t| Online and multiplayer, in the hub and on its own | 19 |\n1114\t| Dev subfolder, what not to read, shared ports and processes | 4, 24 |\n1115\t| Performance out of scope | 3 |\n1116\t| Testing: headless, scripted playthrough, one command, screenshots only when needed, no review subagents | 24 |\n1117\t| Effort | Yours to spend; 25 and 26 order it |\n1118\t| This computer and its stand-ins | 3, 4, 24 |\n1119\t| Feature-complete vertical slice, infrastructure for later build-out | 4, 14, 25 |\n1120\t| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | 17 |\n1121\t| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits | 18 |\n1122\t| Narrative tone | 15 |\n1123\t| 10-foot controller first, native keyboard and mouse, hover focus, glyphs, Xbox option | 16 |\n1124\t| No dense web-style screens | 16 |\n1125\t| Touch-only notice, settings button, attract runs | 16, 18 |\n1126\t| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | top, 26, 27 |\n1127\t| Source Information, feedback sections, chat reply, About screen | 27 |\n1128\t| Exit interview | 27 |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98060110-4ead-4574-964f-daef0a492581","uuid":"58840a63-a2bb-4027-9162-9f9748a827e8","timestamp":"2026-11-06T13:05:41.894Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162030__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n---\n\n## 25. Build order\n\nEach step ends with a playable build and passing tests, and leaves nothing half-done. Do them in order. Write sound, feedback and tests with each feature rather than at the end.\n\n| Step | Do | Done when |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 Pipeline | Pick the title. Unzip the kit. Download `vendor/`. `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs` with the hub suite. A blank `createGame()` with a shared-world round that does nothing | Single file opens on `file://`; **all four hub scenarios pass from day one**. Keep them passing |\n| 1 Walk | Terrain, roads, river, sky, wind, grass, trees. Player, `installLook`, actions, pad codes, device tracking, HUD basics, pause | You can walk the Kettle by pad or mouse |\n| 2 Shoot | Hands rig and `tractor` first, soldier rig, one AI squad, damage, health, syrette, wear, jam, reload. SFX for each | A fight at P2 is fun on its own. Add the other guns |\n| 3 Burn | Fire grid, flare, bottle, flamethrower, scars, regrowth, soldier panic, day and night. Fire sounds | Lighting a field at night makes you stop and watch |\n| 4 Drive | Vehicles, driving view, damage, repair, patrols, posts, train | Run a checkpoint in a pickup and break down after |\n| 5 World | All sites, safehouses, Tibor, stones, tracker, fever, tablets, surgery, monocular, held map, rail halts, trig points, save and load | A whole session without missions works |\n| 6 Story | Mission runner, dialogue, voices, buddies, rescues, M0 to End, Ledger, endings. Playthrough suite | A bot and you both finish it |\n| 7 Shell | Title, all menus, settings, glyph sets, About, touch gate, attract, dev mode | Every screen works by pad, keyboard and mouse |\n| 8 Multiplayer | Sim/view puppets, host, client, sessions, Cash Run, card, solo bots, link | Hub and link suites pass with a real round |\n| 9 Polish | All music, ambience, feel, art passes, balance with the sliders, screenshots | You'd show it to someone |\n| 10 Ship | Final build, About pages, exit interview, chat reply, `node tests/run.mjs --all` | Section 27 is all true |\n\n---\n\n## 26. Cut order\n\nIf you can't finish everything, cut whole things from the top of this list. Don't leave stubs, and remove all traces (menu entries, map marks, lines). Keep every feature in the demo reachable and shown.\n\n1. Fauna (vultures first).\n2. Same-computer link (keep hub and solo).\n3. The game's own hub card (use the hub's card: `card:false`).\n4. Chase camera; dust weather.\n5. Mercy Runs after the first; trig points down to 3; cases down to 6.\n6. M5 (Mags offers her alternate plan in M6 instead: get the field workers out first, for less pay).\n7. M4 (give the tracker in M3; use the MG in M7; Dov joins at the bar).\n8. M8 (the column just starts walking after M7).\n9. Shared-world → ghost → parallel (section 19).\n\nNever cut: fire, wear and jams, fever, the held map, buddy rescue and the finish-it choice, Harrow's twist, dev mode with sliders, attract mode, glyphs and native mouse, touch gate, the hub check, music at 10%, the voices.\n\n---\n\n## 27. Done, reports, coverage\n\n**Done means:** every line below is true and you've checked it.\n- [ ] Your title, chosen by the brief's procedure, appears on the title screen and in `<title>`.\n- [ ] The one HTML file runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it.\n- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs --all` reports 0 failed.\n- [ ] All four hub scenarios pass, and the game still runs outside a session.\n- [ ] You finished the game yourself by bot, both endings.\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features anywhere.\n- [ ] Every screen shows native prompts for pad (PlayStation and Xbox glyphs) and keyboard/mouse.\n- [ ] You looked at the screenshots.\n- [ ] `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the copy in the kit's zip (keep the zip in `vendor/` and compare in the `static` suite).\n\n**About and Credits screen** (in-game, paged, reached from main menu and pause; text in `src/content/about.js`). Your brief marks this as critical. It must show:\n- **Source Information,** with every field your brief lists: Date (from your brief), Source (Far Cry 2), Plan (the file name of this plan as you received it), Genre and Perspective, Tech (engine and its version, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, esbuild, your additions), Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model (from your brief), and Usage: *added on import*. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback:** your brief's questions and your honest answers, in order, each on its own page. Don't estimate cost.\n- **Credits:** Pixel Engine (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), the AI Hub session client, and \"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\".\n\n**Your final chat reply** repeats the same Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback.\n\n**The exit interview file** your brief specifies goes beside the HTML, in the exact JSON shape it gives, every value filled, and must parse. Suggested catalogue values: type `Game`; genre `Shooter`; source `Far Cry 2`; series `Far Cry`; players `1–4`; tags such as `first-person`, `pixel art`, `co-op`, `versus`, `vehicles`, `story`; the name is your title.\n\n**Where each line of your brief is covered:**\n\n| Brief item | Section |\n| --- | --- |\n| Source, original game, capture world, characters, themes, tone | 1, 2, 15 |\n| Genre, perspective | 1 |\n| Title by random pick (left to you) | top, 25 |\n| Research | 3 (done for you; research more if you want) |\n| Single HTML package, CDN allowance, bundle into root | 23 |\n| Engine kit kept as shipped, systems added on top | 3, 4, 23 |\n| Art direction: native base, new assets, library for generic props | 20 |\n| Animation: native base, new animations | 21 |\n| Audio in code, Animalese voices, music 10% | 22 |\n| Online and multiplayer, in the hub and on its own | 19 |\n| Dev subfolder, what not to read, shared ports and processes | 4, 24 |\n| Performance out of scope | 3 |\n| Testing: headless, scripted playthrough, one command, screenshots only when needed, no review subagents | 24 |\n| Effort | Yours to spend; 25 and 26 order it |\n| This computer and its stand-ins | 3, 4, 24 |\n| Feature-complete vertical slice, infrastructure for later build-out | 4, 14, 25 |\n| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | 17 |\n| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits | 18 |\n| Narrative tone | 15 |\n| 10-foot controller first, native keyboard and mouse, hover focus, glyphs, Xbox option | 16 |\n| No dense web-style screens | 16 |\n| Touch-only notice, settings button, attract runs | 16, 18 |\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | top, 26, 27 |\n| Source Information, feedback sections, chat reply, About screen | 27 |\n| Exit interview | 27 |","numLines":92,"startLine":1037,"totalLines":1129}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FrZUWCGeZHiKUwgSMx7ofS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
